var e={"musculoskeletal-low-back-pain":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Low back pain** is pain between the **costal margins and the inferior gluteal folds**, with or without leg pain.`,`**Acute** is under 6 weeks, **sub-acute** 6-12 weeks and **chronic** over 12 weeks.`,`About half improve within 1 week and **90% within 6 weeks**.`,`Recurrence within a year is **40-60%**, so counsel recovery with recurrence, not cure.`,`It is the **leading cause of years lived with disability** worldwide.`,`Degenerative changes on imaging are common in **asymptomatic adults over 40** and mean ageing, not damage.`,`**Yellow flags** (fear-avoidance, catastrophising, low mood, compensation) predict chronicity, not structure.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Triage every patient into one of **three boxes** at the first visit.`,`**Non-specific mechanical pain** makes up over 90% of cases.`,`**Radicular pain or radiculopathy (sciatica)** makes up 5-10%.`,`**Serious specific pathology** makes up 1-2%: cauda equina, infection, cancer, fracture, axial SpA.`,`Radicular pain radiates **below the knee** in a dermatomal pattern; facet or SI pain stops above it.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`The disc dries with age: proteoglycan falls, disc height drops and the annulus develops **radial fissures**.`,`Nerve fibres grow into fissured discs, so the disc itself becomes a pain generator.`,`Prolapse goes **posterolateral** because the posterior longitudinal ligament is weak laterally.`,`An **L4-L5 disc hits the L5 root**; an **L5-S1 disc hits the S1 root**.`,`Extruded nucleus releases **TNF-alpha, IL-1 and phospholipase A2**, chemically inflaming the root.`,`Macrophages resorb the fragment, so about **75% of sciatica settles in 6-12 weeks**.`,`Chronic pain is driven by **central sensitisation**, which is why exercise, CBT and duloxetine help.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask if pain radiates **below the knee** with numbness or tingling.`,`Ask **bladder, bowel and saddle sensation** in every patient with leg symptoms.`,`Red-flag sweep: age under 20 or over 50, **fever, night sweats, weight loss**, night pain, known cancer.`,`Always ask **TB contact, past TB**, diabetes, HIV and long-term steroids.`,`**Inflammatory back pain**: onset before 40-45, stiffness over 30 minutes, better with exercise.`,`Leg pain on walking relieved by **sitting or bending forward** means neurogenic claudication.`,`Record a functional baseline: walking distance, sitting tolerance, work days lost.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Look for scoliosis, a **gibbus**, a palpable step and a cold abscess.`,`Cannot **heel-walk** means L5 weakness; cannot **toe-walk** means S1 weakness.`,`**SLR** is positive if leg pain below the knee appears between **30 and 70 degrees**.`,`SLR is sensitive; **crossed SLR** is highly specific for a large disc prolapse.`,`**Femoral stretch test** detects L2-L4 root lesions that the SLR misses.`,`Test **saddle sensation and anal tone** whenever bladder or bilateral leg symptoms exist.`,`Examine hips, sacroiliac joints, abdomen for an **aortic aneurysm** and peripheral pulses.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**No imaging for non-specific back pain** without red flags.`,`Plain X-ray for suspected fracture or spondylolisthesis.`,`**Same-day MRI** for suspected cauda equina syndrome.`,`MRI also for infection, malignancy and progressive neurological deficit.`,`**ESR, CRP and CBC** when infection or cancer is suspected.`,`Pott spine: MRI then **CT-guided biopsy for CBNAAT** and culture before treatment.`,`Suspected axial SpA: CRP, **HLA-B27**, X-ray pelvis, then MRI sacroiliac joints.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Reassure**: a mechanical strain of a strong spine, with an excellent prognosis.`,`**Stay active**; avoid bed rest beyond 1-2 days and return early to modified work.`,`Superficial heat for 15-20 minutes helps pain and spasm.`,`**Ibuprofen 400 mg TDS** or **naproxen 250-500 mg BD** for 5-7 days after food.`,`Add a **PPI** if over 65, past ulcer, or on aspirin, anticoagulant, steroid or SSRI.`,`**Paracetamol alone is no better than placebo**; use it only as an add-on.`,`Stratify with **STarT Back** at the first visit to choose the care pathway.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**Exercise for 8-12 weeks** (core, walking, yoga) beats every drug for chronic pain.`,`Manual therapy only alongside exercise; add **CBT** for high-risk patients.`,`Spasm: **tizanidine 2-4 mg** at night for 3-7 days; never with ciprofloxacin.`,`Severe acute pain: **tramadol 50 mg** 6-8 hourly for under 1-2 weeks only.`,`Chronic pain: **duloxetine 30 mg** for 1 week, then 60 mg (ACP).`,`**No gabapentinoids, oral steroids or benzodiazepines** for sciatica (NICE).`,`Do not offer belts, traction, TENS, ultrasound or repeated IM injections.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`**Cauda equina syndrome**: bilateral sciatica, saddle anaesthesia, bladder dysfunction, lax anal tone.`,`Grades are **CES-S, CES-I and CES-R**; outcome is best if operated at CES-I.`,`Fever with focal tenderness in a diabetic suggests a **spinal epidural abscess**.`,`Back pain in known cancer: **dexamethasone 16 mg** and same-day MRI whole spine.`,`Sudden severe back pain with collapse may be a **leaking aortic aneurysm**.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review at **1-2 weeks**; reassess the triage box at **4-6 weeks**.`,`Refer same day for cauda equina, **power 3/5 or less**, infection, cancer or unstable fracture.`,`Refer routinely for radicular pain persisting beyond **6-12 weeks**.`,`Discectomy relieves leg pain faster but outcomes are **similar at 1-2 years**.`,`No spinal fusion for non-specific low back pain outside a trial.`,`Give a written **flare plan**: NSAID for 3-5 days, heat, keep moving.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for low back pain`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Ibuprofen`,`400 mg oral TDS, max 2.4 g/day`,`5-7 days`,`GI bleed, AKI, BP rise; PPI if at risk`],[`Naproxen`,`250-500 mg oral BD`,`5-7 days`,`As ibuprofen; lowest CV risk of NSAIDs`],[`Etoricoxib / celecoxib`,`60-90 mg OD / 100-200 mg BD oral`,`Short course`,`MI, stroke, oedema; avoid in IHD`],[`Paracetamol`,`500 mg-1 g oral 6-hourly, max 4 g/day`,`Add-on only`,`Liver toxicity; 3 g max if under 50 kg`],[`Tizanidine`,`2-4 mg oral at night, up to TDS`,`3-7 days`,`Drowsiness, hypotension; never with ciprofloxacin`],[`Tramadol`,`50 mg oral 6-8 hourly`,`Under 1-2 weeks`,`Seizures, serotonin syndrome with SSRIs`],[`Duloxetine`,`30 mg OD for 1 week, then 60 mg`,`Review at 8-12 weeks`,`Nausea, sweating, BP rise; taper to stop`],[`Amitriptyline`,`10 mg at night, up to 25-50 mg`,`Neuropathic leg pain`,`Drowsiness, retention, falls; avoid after MI`],[`Epidural steroid`,`Triamcinolone or methylprednisolone 40-80 mg`,`Single injection`,`Specialist; short-term relief only`]]},{heading:`Lumbar root levels`,columns:[`Root (disc)`,`Sensory loss`,`Weakness`,`Reflex`],rows:[[`L4 (L3-L4)`,`Anterior thigh, medial leg`,`Knee extension`,`Knee jerk reduced`],[`L5 (L4-L5)`,`Lateral leg, first web space`,`Great toe extension, foot drop`,`Reflexes preserved`],[`S1 (L5-S1)`,`Lateral border and sole of foot`,`Plantarflexion; cannot toe-walk`,`Ankle jerk reduced`],[`Cauda equina`,`Saddle area, both legs`,`Bilateral, variable`,`Lax anal tone`]]},{heading:`Mechanical vs inflammatory vs neurogenic claudication`,columns:[`Feature`,`Mechanical`,`Inflammatory (axial SpA)`,`Neurogenic claudication`],rows:[[`Age at onset`,`Any, often 30-55`,`Under 40-45`,`Over 60`],[`Morning stiffness`,`Under 30 minutes`,`Over 30 minutes, night pain`,`Not prominent`],[`Exercise`,`Worse with loading`,`Better with exercise`,`Leg pain on walking upright`],[`Relief`,`Rest`,`Movement, NSAID`,`Sitting or bending forward`],[`Key test`,`None needed`,`CRP, HLA-B27, MRI SI joints`,`MRI lumbar spine`]]},{heading:`STarT Back risk groups`,columns:[`Score`,`Risk group`,`Care`],rows:[[`Total 0-3`,`Low`,`Advice and analgesia in primary care`],[`Total 4+, psychosocial 3 or less`,`Medium`,`Physiotherapy-led exercise`],[`Psychosocial subscore 4+`,`High`,`Psychologically informed physiotherapy`]]}],remember:[`Over 90% is non-specific: triage and red flags, not an X-ray.`,`Reassure and stay active; bed rest beyond 1-2 days harms.`,`SLR is sensitive; crossed SLR is specific.`,`L4-L5 disc hits L5 (big toe); L5-S1 disc hits S1 (ankle jerk).`,`Neurogenic claudication eases on sitting or bending forward; pulses are normal.`,`No gabapentinoids, oral steroids or benzodiazepines for sciatica.`,`Young adult, evening fever and gibbus: Pott spine; get tissue for CBNAAT first.`]},master:{heading:`Master revision table - Low back pain`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Pain **between the costal margins and the inferior gluteal folds**, with or without leg pain; **acute under 6 weeks, sub-acute 6-12, chronic over 12 weeks**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Non-specific mechanical over 90%**, **radicular pain/radiculopathy 5-10%**, **serious specific pathology 1-2%** (cauda equina, infection incl. **Pott spine**, cancer, fracture, axial SpA).`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Lifting, twisting, vibration (two-wheeler commutes), prolonged sitting, smoking, obesity; **yellow flags** (fear-avoidance, catastrophising, low mood, compensation) predict chronicity, not structure.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Disc dehydration -> annular fissures -> **posterolateral prolapse** (L4-5 hits **L5**, L5-S1 hits **S1**) -> chemical root inflammation (TNF, PLA2) -> sciatica; chronic pain = **central sensitisation**.`],[`Key history`,`Radiation **below the knee**, posture effect, **stiffness over 30 min/night pain** (inflammatory), **bladder, bowel, saddle sensation**, fever, weight loss, cancer, TB contact, steroids, progressive weakness.`],[`Key examination`,`Gait, heel walk (L5) and toe walk (S1), **SLR 30-70 degrees** (sensitive), **crossed SLR** (specific), femoral stretch (L2-4), root-wise power/reflexes, **saddle sensation and anal tone**, Schober, gibbus.`],[`Investigations`,`**No imaging without red flags**; X-ray for fracture/listhesis; **same-day MRI for cauda equina**, MRI for infection, cancer, progressive deficit; ESR/CRP/CBC; **CT-guided biopsy for CBNAAT** in Pott spine.`],[`Risk stratification (STarT Back)`,`**STarT Back**: total **0-3 low** (advice), **4+ with psychosocial subscore 3 or less medium** (physio), **psychosocial 4+ high** (psychologically informed physio).`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Reassure, stay active, no bed rest beyond 1-2 days**, heat; **ibuprofen 400 mg TDS** or **naproxen 250-500 mg BD** for 5-7 days with PPI if at risk; paracetamol only as add-on.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Exercise/yoga **8-12 weeks**, manual therapy only with exercise, CBT; tizanidine 2-4 mg short course for spasm; **tramadol 50 mg** under 1-2 weeks; chronic - **duloxetine 30 then 60 mg** (ACP).`],[`Treatment - avoid`,`**No gabapentinoids, oral steroids or benzodiazepines for sciatica** (NICE); no opioids for chronic LBP; no belts, traction, TENS; **thiocolchicoside max 8 mg 12-hourly for 7 days**, avoid in pregnancy.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`**Epidural steroid** (triamcinolone or methylprednisolone 40-80 mg) for acute severe sciatica - short-term only; **medial branch RF denervation** for facet pain; discectomy = faster relief, same 1-2 year outcome.`],[`Special groups`,`**Pregnancy** - paracetamol, no NSAID from 20 weeks; **elderly/CKD** - paracetamol, topical NSAID, half-dose tramadol, avoid amitriptyline in frail; young with evening fever - **Pott spine**.`],[`Complications`,`Chronic disabling pain, opioid dependence, **cauda equina with permanent sphincter loss**, foot drop, paraplegia from Pott spine or metastasis.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`**CES-S/I/R** - same-day MRI and decompression; progressive deficit (power 3/5 or less), fever with tenderness (epidural abscess), cancer (**dexamethasone 16 mg**, same-day MRI), AAA; routine referral if radicular pain over 6-12 weeks.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Review **1-2 weeks**; reassess triage box at **4-6 weeks**; track function (walking, sitting, work), neurology and NSAID side effects; written flare plan.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`Ergonomics (load close to body, knees bent, no twisting), break sitting every 30-45 min, **weight loss, stop smoking**, maintenance exercise; written flare plan; belts and back schools do not prevent.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`No change in this pass: NICE NG59 (2020), ACP 2017 and **WHO 2023 chronic LBP guideline** (exercise, structured education; no routine opioids) remain current.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Over 90% non-specific - **triage, not X-ray**; crossed SLR is specific; neurogenic claudication eases on **sitting/bending forward**; Pott spine needs **tissue for CBNAAT before ATT** (about 12 months).`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Back pain in the OPD: triage in five minutes`,steps:[{label:`History and exam with red-flag sweep`,detail:`Bladder, saddle, fever, weight loss, cancer, TB, steroids, trauma`},{label:`Cauda equina features?`,detail:`Retention, saddle anaesthesia, bilateral sciatica, lax anal tone`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Yes - same-day MRI and spinal surgeon`,detail:`Bladder scan; decompress early (CES-I best)`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Other red flag (infection, cancer, fracture)?`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Urgent MRI, ESR/CRP, biopsy for CBNAAT`,detail:`Dexamethasone 16 mg if cord compression`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Leg pain below knee - radicular`,detail:`NSAID, stay active; review 6-12 weeks; MRI if surgery planned`},{label:`Non-specific - STarT Back stratify`,detail:`Advice, NSAID 5-7 days, exercise; no imaging`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Non-specific low back pain: treatment ladder`,steps:[{label:`Reassurance, stay active, heat`,detail:`No bed rest over 1-2 days; return to modified work`},{label:`Short NSAID course`,detail:`Ibuprofen 400 mg TDS or naproxen 250-500 mg BD, 5-7 days; PPI if over 65 or ulcer risk`},{label:`Spasm: short muscle relaxant`,detail:`Tizanidine 2-4 mg at night for 3-7 days; never with ciprofloxacin`},{label:`Exercise programme 8-12 weeks`,detail:`Core, walking, yoga; manual therapy only with exercise; CBT if high risk`},{label:`Chronic: duloxetine (ACP)`,detail:`30 mg OD 1 week then 60 mg; no opioids, no gabapentinoids for sciatica`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Specialist procedures`,detail:`Epidural steroid for severe sciatica; RF denervation for facet pain; discectomy`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Mechanical vs inflammatory vs neurogenic claudication`,columns:[`Feature`,`Mechanical`,`Inflammatory (axial SpA)`,`Neurogenic claudication`],rows:[[`Age at onset`,`Any, often 30-55`,`Under 40-45`,`Over 60`],[`Morning stiffness`,`Under 30 min`,`Over 30 min, night pain`,`Not prominent`],[`Effect of exercise`,`Worse with loading`,`Better with exercise`,`Leg pain on walking upright`],[`Relief`,`Rest`,`Movement, NSAID in 48 h`,`Sitting or bending forward`],[`Key test`,`None needed`,`CRP, HLA-B27, MRI SI joints`,`MRI lumbar spine`]]}]},"musculoskeletal-knee-osteoarthritis":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Osteoarthritis** is a disease of the **whole joint**, not simple wear and tear.`,`It involves cartilage loss, subchondral sclerosis and cysts, osteophytes and synovitis.`,`**Clinical diagnosis (NICE)**: age 45 or over with **activity-related pain**.`,`Morning stiffness is absent or **30 minutes or less**; no imaging or blood test is needed.`,`Symptomatic knee OA affects **20-30% of Indian adults over 45**.`,`**Pain correlates poorly with the X-ray**, so treat symptoms and function, not the film.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Kellgren-Lawrence grades 0-4**; grade 2 is definite radiographic OA.`,`The grade **never decides treatment or surgery**; symptoms and function do.`,`**Medial compartment OA with varus** is the commonest pattern in India.`,`**Patellofemoral OA** gives pain on stairs and rising, seen only on a skyline view.`,`**Secondary OA** follows meniscal or ACL injury, fracture, inflammatory or septic arthritis.`]},{title:`Causes and risk factors`,points:[`Age, female sex and **obesity** are the main risk factors.`,`Each 1 kg of body weight adds about **3-4 kg of force** across the knee.`,`**Varus knees**, squatting and floor sitting load the medial compartment.`,`Past **meniscectomy or ACL tear** explains early or one-sided OA.`,`**Quadriceps weakness** removes the knee's shock absorber.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Abnormal load on the joint switches chondrocytes to a **catabolic state**.`,`**IL-1beta and TNF-alpha** induce MMP-13 and ADAMTS-5, which digest the cartilage matrix.`,`Aggrecan is lost first, then collagen, giving fissures and full-thickness loss.`,`Subchondral bone thickens (**sclerosis**) and forms cysts under load.`,`**Osteophytes** grow at the margins, causing bony enlargement and crepitus.`,`Cartilage has no nerves; pain comes from **bone, synovium and capsule**.`,`Effusion causes **reflex quadriceps inhibition**, which loads the joint further.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Pain is **worse with use**, on stairs and rising from the floor, better with rest.`,`Stiffness **over 60 minutes** points to inflammatory arthritis, not OA.`,`Ask about **squatting, cross-legged sitting and stairs**; these define disability.`,`True locking suggests a loose body or a displaced meniscal tear.`,`Ask about ulcer, CKD, heart disease and **aspirin, anticoagulant or SSRI** before any NSAID.`,`A **hot acute knee** is gout, pseudogout or sepsis until aspirated.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Look for **varus or valgus**, quadriceps wasting and a lateral thrust on walking.`,`Feel for warmth; warmth means inflammation or sepsis, not simple OA.`,`**Joint-line tenderness**, coarse crepitus and a **bulge test** for small effusions.`,`Check for **fixed flexion deformity** by pressing the knee flat.`,`**Always examine the hip**, because hip OA refers pain to the knee.`,`Timed **chair stand** and walking measure function and guide referral.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**No test is needed** to diagnose typical knee OA at 45 or over.`,`If imaging is needed, use **weight-bearing AP, lateral and skyline views**.`,`A supine film **hides joint-space loss**.`,`**Aspirate any hot or tense effusion** for cells, Gram stain, culture and crystals.`,`Before oral NSAIDs, check **creatinine, potassium and BP**.`,`Do not order routine MRI, rheumatoid factor or uric acid.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Education, exercise and weight loss** are the core for every patient.`,`**Quadriceps strengthening** daily for at least 8-12 weeks is the best single treatment.`,`Add low-impact aerobic activity, building to **150 minutes a week**.`,`**Weight loss of 5-10%** gives meaningful pain relief.`,`Use a Western commode, raised chair and a **stick in the opposite hand**.`,`**Topical NSAID** (diclofenac 1% gel) is the first-line drug for knee OA.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Flares: **ibuprofen 400 mg TDS** or **naproxen 250-500 mg BD**, shortest course.`,`**Always add a PPI** with an oral NSAID in OA (NICE).`,`**Intra-articular triamcinolone 40 mg** for a flare with effusion; relief lasts 2-8 weeks.`,`Maximum **3-4 injections a year** in one joint.`,`**Duloxetine** for chronic pain with central sensitisation or depression.`,`NICE no longer recommends **routine paracetamol or weak opioids**.`,`No glucosamine, hyaluronic acid, PRP or arthroscopic washout.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review at **4-6 weeks**, then every 3-6 months for function, weight and exercise.`,`On regular NSAIDs, check **BP, creatinine and potassium** every 3-6 months.`,`Refer for **knee replacement** when pain and function fail despite non-surgical care.`,`Never refer on radiographic grade, age or BMI alone.`,`Young patients with **isolated medial OA** may suit high tibial osteotomy.`,`Refer urgently for a **hot knee with fever** or a true locked knee.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for knee osteoarthritis`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Diclofenac 1% gel`,`2-4 g topical 3-4 times daily`,`Reassess at 2-4 weeks`,`Local rash; wash hands after`],[`Capsaicin 0.025-0.075% cream`,`Topical 3-4 times daily`,`Benefit after 2-4 weeks`,`Burning first 1-2 weeks; avoid eyes`],[`Ibuprofen`,`400 mg oral TDS after food`,`Flares, shortest course`,`GI bleed, AKI, BP rise; give PPI`],[`Naproxen`,`250-500 mg oral BD after food`,`Flares, shortest course`,`Lowest CV risk; avoid eGFR under 30`],[`Etoricoxib / celecoxib`,`30-60 mg OD / 200 mg OD oral`,`Shortest course`,`MI, stroke, oedema; avoid in IHD`],[`Paracetamol`,`500 mg-1 g up to QID oral`,`Short, infrequent use`,`Max 3 g/day in elderly`],[`Triamcinolone acetonide`,`40 mg intra-articular with lignocaine`,`3-4 per year maximum`,`Post-injection flare, sugar rise, sepsis`],[`Duloxetine`,`30 mg OD for 1 week, then 60 mg`,`Review at 8-12 weeks`,`Nausea, sweating, BP rise; taper`],[`Tramadol`,`50 mg oral 6-8 hourly`,`Short term only`,`Falls, confusion, serotonin syndrome`]]},{heading:`Kellgren-Lawrence grades`,columns:[`Grade`,`X-ray features`,`Meaning`],rows:[[`0`,`No features of OA`,`Normal`],[`1`,`Doubtful narrowing, possible osteophytic lipping`,`Doubtful OA`],[`2`,`Definite osteophytes, possible narrowing`,`Definite OA threshold`],[`3`,`Moderate osteophytes, definite narrowing, sclerosis`,`Moderate OA`],[`4`,`Large osteophytes, marked narrowing, bone deformity`,`Severe OA`]]},{heading:`OA vs inflammatory vs septic knee`,columns:[`Feature`,`Osteoarthritis`,`Inflammatory arthritis`,`Septic or crystal`],rows:[[`Morning stiffness`,`30 minutes or less`,`Over 60 minutes`,`Variable`],[`Warmth`,`Cool or slightly warm`,`Warm, boggy synovitis`,`Hot, red, tense`],[`Other joints`,`Knees, hips, hand DIPs`,`Small hand joints, wrists`,`Usually one joint`],[`Joint fluid`,`Clear, under 2000 cells`,`Inflammatory`,`Turbid; crystals or organisms`],[`Action`,`Core treatment in primary care`,`Refer to rheumatology`,`Aspirate and refer same day`]]}],remember:[`Clinical diagnosis: 45 or over, activity pain, stiffness 30 minutes or less.`,`Education, exercise and weight loss first in every answer.`,`Each kg of body weight adds 3-4 kg across the knee.`,`Topical NSAID first; always a PPI with an oral NSAID.`,`Triple whammy: NSAID plus ACE inhibitor or ARB plus diuretic - avoid.`,`Weight-bearing films with skyline view; supine films hide narrowing.`,`Refer for replacement on symptoms and function, never on K-L grade alone.`]},master:{heading:`Master revision table - Knee osteoarthritis`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Whole-joint disease** - focal cartilage loss, subchondral sclerosis/cysts, osteophytes, synovitis; clinical diagnosis at **45 or over with activity-related pain and morning stiffness 30 min or less** (NICE).`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Kellgren-Lawrence 0-4** (grade 2 = definite OA) - never decides surgery; compartments: **medial (varus, commonest in India)**, lateral, patellofemoral; primary vs secondary.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Age, female sex, **obesity**, varus, prior meniscal/ACL injury, squatting and floor sitting, quadriceps weakness, inflammatory or crystal arthritis.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Abnormal load -> catabolic chondrocytes -> **IL-1, MMP-13, ADAMTS-5** -> aggrecan then collagen loss -> sclerosis, osteophytes (TGF-beta), synovitis -> pain from bone, synovium, capsule.`],[`Key history`,`Pain on **stairs, rising, squatting**, gelling, stiffness under 30 min; locking/giving way; Indian tasks (**toilet squat, floor sitting**); NSAID-risk drug history, CKD, IHD.`],[`Key examination`,`Varus, **quadriceps wasting** (girth 15 cm above patella), cool effusion (bulge/tap), **coarse crepitus**, joint-line tenderness, fixed flexion; **always examine the hip**.`],[`Investigations`,`**None needed** for typical OA; **weight-bearing AP + lateral + skyline** if atypical or before surgery; aspirate hot effusion (OA fluid under 2000 WBC); baseline creatinine before NSAIDs.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`NICE clinical (45+, activity pain, stiffness 30 min or less); **ACR 1986 clinical**: knee pain + 3 of 6 (age over 50, stiffness under 30 min, crepitus, bony tenderness, enlargement, no warmth).`],[`Treatment - core (everyone)`,`**Education, quadriceps-strengthening exercise 8-12 weeks, 5-10% weight loss**; walking stick in opposite hand, Western commode, avoid deep squatting.`],[`Treatment - first line (drug)`,`**Topical diclofenac 1% gel 2-4 g 3-4 times daily** (max 16 g/day), reassess at 2-4 weeks; capsaicin cream option; **paracetamol not routine** (NICE 2022).`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`**Oral naproxen 250-500 mg BD or ibuprofen 400 mg TDS with PPI**, shortest time; COX-2 (etoricoxib 30-60 mg) if GI risk but not CV risk; **duloxetine 30 then 60 mg** for sensitisation.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`**Intra-articular triamcinolone 40 mg + 2-4 mL lignocaine**, superolateral, aseptic; relief 2-8 weeks; **max 3-4/year**; avoid within 3 months of arthroplasty; genicular RF ablation if unfit.`],[`Treatment - avoid`,`**No glucosamine, chondroitin, diacerein, hyaluronic acid, PRP, stem cells, lateral wedge insoles, arthroscopic washout**; no routine opioids (NICE).`],[`Special groups`,`**CKD/heart disease/elderly** - topical NSAID, IA steroid, exercise; oral naproxen only with PPI and creatinine at 1-2 weeks; **pregnancy** - no oral NSAID from 20 weeks.`],[`Complications`,`Disability, falls, NSAID harm (**GI bleed, AKI, triple whammy**), Baker cyst rupture (mimics DVT), fixed flexion deformity.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`**Hot knee with fever - septic arthritis**; true locking; night/rest pain or mass; refer for **arthroplasty on symptoms and function despite non-surgical care**, not on KL grade, age or BMI.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Review **4-6 weeks** then 3-6 monthly: function (stairs, squat, chair stand), weight, adherence; on oral NSAID - **BP, creatinine, K at 2-4 weeks, then 3-6 monthly**.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`No change in this pass: NICE NG226 (2022), OARSI 2019, ACR 2019 and EULAR 2023 core-management remain current.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Each kg adds **3-4 kg across the knee**; **topical NSAID first line**; always PPI with oral NSAID; weight-bearing film with skyline; examine the hip in every knee.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Knee pain over 45: diagnosis and first decisions`,steps:[{label:`Activity pain, stiffness 30 min or less?`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Hot joint, fever or tense effusion?`,detail:`Aspirate: Gram stain, culture, crystals`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Clinical OA - no X-ray needed`,detail:`X-ray only if atypical or pre-surgery`},{label:`Core: education, exercise, weight loss`,detail:`Quadriceps programme 8-12 weeks; lose 5-10%`},{label:`Topical NSAID, then oral NSAID + PPI`,detail:`Check creatinine, BP, K before oral NSAID`},{label:`Function preserved, pain controlled`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Knee OA treatment ladder (NICE 2022)`,steps:[{label:`Education, exercise, weight loss`,detail:`For everyone at every stage`,tone:`good`},{label:`Topical diclofenac 1% gel`,detail:`2-4 g 3-4 times daily, max 16 g/day; reassess 2-4 weeks`},{label:`Oral NSAID with PPI, short course`,detail:`Naproxen 250-500 mg BD or ibuprofen 400 mg TDS + omeprazole 20 mg`},{label:`IA steroid for flares`,detail:`Triamcinolone 40 mg + lignocaine; max 3-4/year`},{label:`Duloxetine for central sensitisation`,detail:`30 mg 1 week then 60 mg daily; review 8-12 weeks`},{label:`Arthroplasty or osteotomy`,detail:`When pain and function fail despite proper non-surgical care`,tone:`warn`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Knee OA vs inflammatory arthritis vs septic or crystal knee`,columns:[`Feature`,`Osteoarthritis`,`Inflammatory (RA, SpA)`,`Septic or crystal`],rows:[[`Onset`,`Gradual, over 45`,`Weeks, often younger`,`Hours to 1-2 days`],[`Morning stiffness`,`30 min or less, gelling`,`Over 30-60 min`,`Constant pain`],[`Joint`,`Cool, bony enlargement, crepitus`,`Warm boggy synovitis, small joints`,`Hot, red, tense effusion`],[`Fluid WBC`,`Under 2,000`,`2,000-50,000`,`Gout/CPPD 2,000-50,000; sepsis usually over 50,000`],[`First step`,`Core care + topical NSAID`,`CRP, RF/anti-CCP, rheumatology`,`Aspirate same day; antibiotics if septic`]]}]},"musculoskeletal-gout":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Gout** is deposition of **monosodium urate (MSU) crystals** in joints and soft tissue.`,`Crystals form when serum urate stays above its **saturation point of 6.8 mg/dL**.`,`**Hyperuricaemia** is urate above 7.0 mg/dL in men and 6.0 mg/dL in women.`,`Most hyperuricaemic people **never get gout**; a high report alone is not a diagnosis.`,`Male to female ratio is about **4:1**; oestrogen is uricosuric.`,`Gout is a **marker of cardiometabolic risk**: hypertension, diabetes, obesity, CKD.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Four stages: **asymptomatic hyperuricaemia, acute flare, intercritical gout, chronic tophaceous gout**.`,`**Primary gout** is mostly renal under-excretion of urate (about 90%).`,`**Secondary gout** follows CKD, diuretics, low-dose aspirin, ciclosporin or pyrazinamide.`,`Overproduction occurs in myeloproliferative disease, psoriasis and tumour lysis.`,`Without crystals, **ACR/EULAR 2015 criteria** classify gout at a score of **8 or more**.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Humans lack uricase, so **xanthine oxidase** makes uric acid as the end product.`,`Urate crystallises in **cool peripheral joints**, so the first MTP is classic.`,`A rise or fall in urate, trauma or dehydration loosens crystals from cartilage.`,`Macrophages swallow the crystals and switch on the **NLRP3 inflammasome**.`,`Caspase-1 releases **IL-1beta**, which brings waves of neutrophils.`,`Neutrophil traps (NETs) end the attack in **7-14 days**.`,`Crystals persist between attacks and dissolve only if urate stays **below 6 mg/dL**.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Pain peaks within **6-24 hours**, often at night; even a bedsheet hurts.`,`**First MTP (podagra)** is involved in 50-70% of first attacks.`,`Attacks resolve completely in 7-14 days with **symptom-free intervals**.`,`Triggers: **alcohol binge**, meat feast, dehydration, fasting, surgery, starting a diuretic.`,`Ask **fever, rigors, prosthesis or immunosuppression** to exclude septic arthritis.`,`Ask about **azathioprine** and colchicine-interacting drugs before prescribing.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Check temperature, pulse and BP first; **septic arthritis** must not be missed.`,`The joint is **red, shiny and exquisitely tender**; swelling may mimic cellulitis.`,`**Peeling skin** over the joint as the attack settles is characteristic.`,`Look for **tophi** on the ear helix, olecranon, Achilles and finger pulps.`,`Measure **BMI, waist and BP**, and look for CKD and heart failure.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Joint aspiration with polarised microscopy** is the gold standard.`,`MSU crystals are **needle-shaped and strongly negatively birefringent**.`,`Always send the same fluid for **Gram stain, cell count and culture**.`,`Urate may be **normal during a flare**; repeat it 2-4 weeks after it settles.`,`Before ULT check **creatinine, LFTs, CBC, HbA1c and lipids**.`,`Ultrasound **double-contour sign** or dual-energy CT when diagnosis is uncertain.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Treat the flare within **24 hours** with colchicine, an NSAID or a steroid.`,`**Colchicine 1 mg**, then **0.5 mg one hour later**, then 0.5 mg BD-TDS.`,`NSAID: **naproxen 750 mg stat**, then 250 mg 8-hourly, with a PPI.`,`**Prednisolone 30-35 mg daily for 5 days** is safest in CKD, heart failure or ulcer.`,`Rest, elevate and apply an **ice pack** for 15-20 minutes.`,`**Never stop allopurinol** during a flare if the patient already takes it.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Start ULT for **2 or more flares a year**, tophi, erosions, CKD 3+, stones or diuretic use.`,`**Allopurinol 100 mg daily** (50 mg in CKD 4-5), up by 100 mg every 2-4 weeks.`,`Target urate **below 6 mg/dL**, and **below 5 mg/dL** with tophi.`,`Give **colchicine 0.5 mg once or twice daily for 3-6 months** as flare cover.`,`**Febuxostat 40-80 mg** if allopurinol fails; avoid in major cardiovascular disease.`,`**Losartan** is the preferred antihypertensive; swap a thiazide if possible.`,`Do not treat **asymptomatic hyperuricaemia** with urate-lowering drugs.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Hot joint with fever is **septic arthritis** until the aspirate proves otherwise.`,`**Allopurinol hypersensitivity** (rash, fever, eosinophilia) usually appears in the first 8 weeks.`,`Stop allopurinol at the **first rash**; mortality reaches 25%.`,`**Colchicine toxicity**: diarrhoea, myopathy, cytopenia, worse with macrolides or azoles.`,`**Allopurinol plus azathioprine** can cause fatal marrow suppression.`,`Urate stones, obstruction and acute urate nephropathy.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review **4-6 weeks** after a flare to recheck urate and agree on ULT.`,`During titration, check **urate every 2-4 weeks**; then every 6-12 months.`,`**Check adherence** at every visit; half stop ULT within a year.`,`Annual BP, weight, HbA1c, lipids and eGFR.`,`Refer if target not reached, **tophaceous gout**, CKD 4-5, or onset under 30.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for gout`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Colchicine (flare)`,`1 mg, then 0.5 mg at 1 h, then BD-TDS`,`Till settled; max 6 mg per course`,`Diarrhoea; avoid macrolides, azoles, eGFR under 30`],[`Naproxen`,`750 mg stat, then 250 mg 8-hourly oral`,`5-7 days`,`GI bleed, AKI; give PPI`],[`Etoricoxib`,`120 mg oral once daily`,`Maximum 8 days`,`CV risk, BP rise; with PPI`],[`Prednisolone`,`30-35 mg oral each morning`,`5 days, no taper`,`High sugars; safest in CKD, ulcer`],[`Intra-articular steroid`,`Methylprednisolone or triamcinolone 40 mg (knee)`,`Single injection`,`Only after sepsis excluded`],[`Allopurinol`,`100 mg daily, up 100 mg every 2-4 weeks`,`Lifelong; usual 300-600 mg, max 900 mg`,`Rash, DRESS, SJS; azathioprine interaction`],[`Febuxostat`,`40 mg daily, then 80 mg if needed`,`Lifelong`,`Raised LFTs; CV death signal (CARES)`],[`Probenecid`,`250 mg BD for 1 week, then 500 mg BD`,`Long term`,`Needs eGFR over 50, no stones`],[`Colchicine (prophylaxis)`,`0.5 mg once or twice daily`,`3-6 months after starting ULT`,`Diarrhoea; myopathy with statins`]]},{heading:`Synovial fluid in the hot joint`,columns:[`Type`,`Appearance`,`White cells per microlitre`,`Crystals or organisms`],rows:[[`Non-inflammatory (OA)`,`Clear, viscous`,`200-2,000`,`None`],[`Gout`,`Cloudy`,`2,000-50,000, neutrophils`,`Needle-shaped, strongly negative birefringence`],[`Pseudogout (CPPD)`,`Cloudy`,`2,000-50,000, neutrophils`,`Rhomboid, weakly positive birefringence`],[`Septic`,`Turbid, purulent`,`Usually over 50,000`,`Organisms on Gram stain or culture`]]},{heading:`Choosing the flare drug`,columns:[`Patient`,`Preferred`,`Avoid`],rows:[[`Normal kidneys and stomach`,`Colchicine or NSAID with PPI`,`-`],[`CKD or heart failure`,`Prednisolone or intra-articular steroid`,`NSAID; colchicine if eGFR under 30`],[`Peptic ulcer or anticoagulant`,`Prednisolone or colchicine`,`NSAID`],[`Diabetes`,`Colchicine or intra-articular steroid`,`High-dose oral steroid`],[`On macrolide, azole or verapamil`,`Prednisolone or NSAID`,`Colchicine`]]}],remember:[`The crystal makes the diagnosis, not the number.`,`MSU: needle-shaped, negative; CPPD: rhomboid, positive ("P for Pseudogout, Positive").`,`Every hot joint is septic until the aspirate says otherwise.`,`Low-dose colchicine: 1 mg, then 0.5 mg an hour later.`,`Never stop allopurinol in a flare; start low, go slow, treat to target.`,`Fixed allopurinol 300 mg without checking urate is the commonest error.`,`Allopurinol plus azathioprine can kill.`,`Losartan is the antihypertensive of choice in gout.`]},master:{heading:`Master revision table - Gout`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Monosodium urate crystal deposition** once urate stays above **6.8 mg/dL**; hyperuricaemia = over 7.0 (men), 6.0 (women) - most never get gout.`],[`Relevant classification`,`Stages: **asymptomatic hyperuricaemia, acute flare, intercritical, chronic tophaceous**; **ACR/EULAR 2015 criteria** - 8 or more of 23 points; crystals are the gold standard.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`**Under-excretion 90%**; CKD, **thiazide/loop diuretics, low-dose aspirin**, ciclosporin, pyrazinamide; beer, spirits, fructose drinks, red meat, seafood; obesity, metabolic syndrome.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Urate over saturation -> MSU crystals -> macrophage **NLRP3 inflammasome -> caspase-1 -> IL-1beta** -> neutrophil influx -> flare; NETs end it in 7-14 days.`],[`Key history`,`Peak in **6-24 hours**, night onset, **first MTP (podagra)**, bedsheet intolerable, complete resolution; triggers (alcohol, feast, diuretic, starting ULT); fever/prosthesis (sepsis); azathioprine use.`],[`Key examination`,`Red-purple shiny joint, exquisite tenderness, desquamation; **tophi on helix, olecranon, Achilles**; temperature and sepsis signs; BMI, BP, CKD signs.`],[`Investigations`,`**Aspirate: needle-shaped, strongly negatively birefringent crystals** + Gram stain and culture on same fluid; urate **2-4 weeks after flare**; creatinine, LFT, HbA1c, lipids; ultrasound double contour if unsure.`],[`Treatment - first line (flare)`,`Within 24 h: **colchicine 1 mg then 0.5 mg after 1 h**, then 0.5 mg BD-TDS (max 6 mg/course) **or naproxen 750 mg then 250 mg 8-hourly + PPI** or **prednisolone 30-35 mg for 5 days**.`],[`Choosing the flare drug`,`CKD, heart failure, ulcer, anticoagulation - **prednisolone or IA steroid**; colchicine avoided with clarithromycin, azoles, ciclosporin, verapamil and eGFR under 30; IL-1 blocker (anakinra) as last resort.`],[`Treatment - urate-lowering therapy`,`**Allopurinol 100 mg (50 mg in CKD 4-5), up 100 mg every 2-4 weeks** to urate **under 6 mg/dL (under 5 with tophi)**; usual 300-600, max 900 mg; **do not stop during a flare**.`],[`Indications for ULT`,`**2 or more flares/year, tophus, erosions, CKD 3+, urate stones**, diuretic use, onset under 40 or urate over 8; offer after first flare; **not for asymptomatic hyperuricaemia**.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`**Febuxostat 40 then 80 mg** (avoid in major CV disease - CARES); probenecid 250 mg BD then 500 mg BD (eGFR over 50, no stones); pegloticase specialist (check G6PD).`],[`Flare prophylaxis`,`**Colchicine 0.5 mg OD-BD for 3-6 months** after starting ULT; alternative low-dose NSAID + PPI or prednisolone up to 10 mg.`],[`Special groups`,`**CKD** - prednisolone for flares, allopurinol start 50 mg, febuxostat to eGFR 30; **HLA-B*58:01** test in Han Chinese, Thai, Korean, African ancestry; pregnancy - paracetamol, ice, prednisolone.`],[`Complications`,`Tophi, erosive arthritis, **uric acid stones**, urate nephropathy, **allopurinol hypersensitivity (DRESS/SJS, first 8 weeks)**, colchicine toxicity, cardiovascular disease.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Fever/prosthesis/immunosuppression - **septic arthritis until aspirated**; rash on allopurinol - stop and admit; refer if target missed on max dose, CKD 4-5, tophaceous gout, onset under 30.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Urate and creatinine **every 2-4 weeks while titrating**, then 6-12 monthly; check adherence every visit; annual BP, HbA1c, lipids, eGFR.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`No change in this pass: ACR 2020, NICE NG219 (2022) and EULAR 2016 remain the current gout guidelines (no newer EULAR gout update confirmed).`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Crystal, not number, makes the diagnosis; **losartan** preferred antihypertensive; **allopurinol + azathioprine = marrow aplasia**; fixed 300 mg allopurinol without urate checks is the commonest error.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`The acute hot joint: gout or sepsis`,steps:[{label:`Acute hot swollen joint`},{label:`Fever, prosthesis, immunosuppression, atypical?`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Aspirate same day - culture first`,detail:`Gram stain, cell count, polarised microscopy; antibiotics if septic`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Typical recurrent podagra or MSU crystals`,detail:`Needle-shaped, strongly negatively birefringent`},{label:`Flare drug by comorbidity within 24 h`,detail:`Colchicine low-dose, NSAID + PPI, or prednisolone 30-35 mg x 5 days`},{label:`Continue existing ULT; review 4-6 weeks`,detail:`Urate off-flare, cardiometabolic screen`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Treat-to-target urate lowering`,steps:[{label:`Lifestyle and drug review`,detail:`Cut beer, spirits, fructose drinks; swap thiazide for losartan; keep aspirin`},{label:`Allopurinol start low`,detail:`100 mg daily (50 mg CKD 4-5) with colchicine 0.5 mg OD-BD cover 3-6 months`},{label:`Titrate every 2-4 weeks`,detail:`Up 100 mg (50 mg in CKD) to urate under 6 mg/dL; under 5 with tophi; max 900 mg`},{label:`Febuxostat if intolerant`,detail:`40 mg then 80 mg; avoid in major CV disease`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Add uricosuric or refer`,detail:`Probenecid if eGFR over 50; pegloticase specialist`},{label:`Target held lifelong`,detail:`Tophi shrink over 1-2 years`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Gout vs pseudogout vs septic arthritis`,columns:[`Feature`,`Gout`,`Pseudogout (CPPD)`,`Septic arthritis`],rows:[[`Typical joint`,`First MTP, ankle, knee`,`Knee, wrist in elderly`,`Knee, any; prosthesis`],[`Crystals`,`Needle, strongly negative`,`Rhomboid, weakly positive`,`None (may coexist)`],[`Fluid WBC`,`2,000-50,000`,`2,000-50,000`,`Usually over 50,000`],[`X-ray`,`Punched-out erosions late`,`Chondrocalcinosis`,`Normal early, then destruction`],[`Treatment`,`Colchicine, NSAID, steroid; ULT`,`NSAID, IA steroid`,`IV antibiotics, washout`]]}]},"musculoskeletal-shoulder-pain":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`Shoulder pain comes from four joints, the rotator cuff and bursa, or is **referred**.`,`**Rotator cuff related pain** causes 60-70% of shoulder pain in primary care.`,`**Adhesive capsulitis** is global loss of active and passive movement with a **normal X-ray**.`,`**External rotation is lost first** and most in frozen shoulder.`,`Frozen shoulder peaks at **40-60 years** and affects up to **10-20% of diabetics**.`,`**PMR** is the key bilateral mimic in anyone over 50.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`By structure: **rotator cuff**, adhesive capsulitis, AC joint, glenohumeral OA or instability, referred.`,`Frozen shoulder phase 1, **freezing (painful)**: 2-9 months.`,`Phase 2, **frozen (stiff)**: 4-12 months.`,`Phase 3, **thawing**: 5-24 months; total course about **18-30 months**.`,`The phase decides the plan: **inject early, stretch later**.`,`"Impingement" is now called **rotator cuff related shoulder pain**.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Cuff tendinopathy is **tendinosis** from overload, not true inflammation.`,`Painful arc at **60-120 degrees**: diseased tendon and bursa pass under the acromion.`,`**Progressive loading** makes tenocytes lay down organised collagen.`,`Frozen shoulder starts as **synovitis**, then fibroblasts become myofibroblasts.`,`The **coracohumeral ligament** thickens and capsular volume falls below 10 mL.`,`In diabetes, **glycation end-products** cross-link and stiffen capsular collagen.`,`C5 dermatome and phrenic nerve explain referred pain from neck and diaphragm.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Age guides diagnosis: under 40 instability; **40-60 cuff and frozen shoulder**; over 60 tears, OA, PMR.`,`Cuff pain lies over the **deltoid insertion**; AC pain is pinpointed on top.`,`**Pain below the elbow with tingling** means the neck.`,`Ask **stiffness or weakness**: cannot move it, or moves it but cannot lift it.`,`Ask **diabetes, thyroid disease** and recent surgery or stroke.`,`Exertional left shoulder pain with sweating may be **angina**.`,`Smoker with weight loss or hand wasting suggests a **Pancoast tumour**.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`**Is passive external rotation preserved?** This one question sorts the shoulder.`,`Active painful but passive full means **cuff disease**.`,`Active and passive both lost, external rotation most, means **capsulitis or OA**.`,`Cannot lift actively with full passive range means **tear or nerve lesion**.`,`Jobe empty-can (supraspinatus), lift-off (subscapularis), scarf test (AC joint).`,`Do **Spurling test** and arm neurology in every shoulder case.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Most shoulder pain needs no test** at first presentation.`,`**X-ray** after trauma, any stiff shoulder **over 60**, suspected calcific deposit or red flags.`,`Check **HbA1c** in every new frozen shoulder; add TSH and lipids.`,`**Ultrasound** for suspected full-thickness tear or failed 6-12 weeks of rehab.`,`ESR, CRP and CBC for fever or bilateral girdle pain.`,`No routine MRI; cuff changes are near-universal after 50.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Set expectations: cuff pain settles in **3-6 months**; frozen shoulder in **18-30 months**.`,`Cuff: **relative rest**, no sling, then a **12-week progressive loading** programme.`,`Freezing phase: **pendulum exercises** and pain-free range only.`,`Frozen and thawing phases: stretch **3-5 times daily, holding 20-30 seconds**.`,`**Paracetamol** and a short NSAID course (1-2 weeks) with PPI if at risk.`,`**Amitriptyline 10 mg** at night for sleep-disturbing night pain.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**Intra-articular steroid in the freezing phase** has the best evidence in frozen shoulder.`,`Use **triamcinolone 40 mg with 4-5 mL lignocaine**; relief lasts 6-12 weeks.`,`Subacromial steroid for cuff pain only to **enable exercise**.`,`Maximum **3 injections a year**, at least 6 weeks apart.`,`**Hydrodilatation** if frozen shoulder is not progressing at 3-6 months.`,`**MUA or arthroscopic release** for disabling stiffness after 6-9 months.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`**Diabetics**: longer course, often bilateral; check glucose for 3-7 days after injection.`,`**PMR**: prednisolone 15 mg daily with a dramatic response within a week.`,`PMR with jaw claudication or visual loss is **GCA**: prednisolone 40-60 mg urgently.`,`Over 60: X-ray to exclude glenohumeral OA, which mimics frozen shoulder.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review at **4-6 weeks**; track passive external rotation in degrees.`,`Refer an **acute traumatic cuff tear under 60** within 2 weeks.`,`Cuff pain not better after **3-6 months** of rehab plus up to two injections: refer.`,`Same day: **hot shoulder with fever**, unreduced dislocation, suspected cancer or GCA.`,`Recurrent instability in young athletes needs orthopaedic referral.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs and injections for shoulder pain`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Paracetamol`,`1 g oral QID; max 3 g in elderly`,`Painful phase`,`Liver toxicity in overdose`],[`Ibuprofen / naproxen`,`400 mg TDS / 250-500 mg BD oral`,`1-2 weeks`,`GI bleed, AKI; PPI if over 45`],[`Diclofenac 1% gel`,`2-4 g topical TDS-QID`,`Reassess at 2-4 weeks`,`Local rash; good for AC joint`],[`Amitriptyline`,`10 mg at night, up to 25 mg`,`Review at 4-6 weeks`,`Drowsiness; avoid glaucoma, heart block`],[`Triamcinolone acetonide`,`40 mg with 4-5 mL 1% lignocaine, injected`,`Max 3 a year, 6 weeks apart`,`Flare, high sugar, tendon weakening`],[`Prednisolone (frozen shoulder)`,`30 mg daily, tapered`,`Over 3 weeks`,`Short-lived benefit; avoid uncontrolled diabetes`],[`Prednisolone (PMR)`,`15 mg each morning`,`Slow taper over 1-2 years`,`Needs bone protection`],[`Tramadol`,`50 mg oral up to TDS`,`Few days only`,`Drowsiness, falls; avoid with SSRIs`]]},{heading:`Telling shoulder pain apart`,columns:[`Type`,`Active range`,`Passive range`,`Key sign`],rows:[[`Cuff tendinopathy`,`Painful arc 60-120 degrees`,`Full`,`Neer, Hawkins-Kennedy positive`],[`Full-thickness cuff tear`,`Cannot lift or hold`,`Full`,`Drop-arm, lag sign`],[`Adhesive capsulitis`,`Globally reduced`,`Reduced, ER worst`,`Normal X-ray`],[`Glenohumeral OA`,`Reduced with crepitus`,`Reduced`,`X-ray joint space loss`],[`AC joint disease`,`Pain in last 20-30 degrees`,`Full`,`Scarf test`],[`Calcific tendinopathy`,`Almost nil from pain`,`Restricted by pain`,`X-ray calcific deposit`],[`Referred (neck, heart, apex)`,`Full`,`Full`,`Spurling, ECG, chest X-ray`]]},{heading:`Key shoulder tests`,columns:[`Test`,`Structure`,`Positive means`],rows:[[`Jobe empty-can`,`Supraspinatus`,`Weak: tear; painful: tendinopathy`],[`Drop-arm`,`Supraspinatus`,`Full-thickness tear`],[`External rotation lag`,`Infraspinatus`,`Large posterosuperior tear`],[`Lift-off or belly-press`,`Subscapularis`,`Subscapularis tear`],[`Speed and Yergason`,`Long head of biceps`,`Biceps tendinopathy`],[`Apprehension and relocation`,`Anterior capsule, labrum`,`Anterior instability`]]}],remember:[`Lost passive external rotation with a normal X-ray means frozen shoulder.`,`Cuff: active painful, passive full. Capsulitis: both lost.`,`Painful arc 60-120 degrees is subacromial; last 20-30 degrees is AC joint.`,`Frozen shoulder: freezing, frozen, thawing; tell the patient 18-30 months.`,`HbA1c in every new frozen shoulder.`,`Inject early in freezing; stretch hard in frozen and thawing.`,`Pain below the elbow with tingling is the neck until proven otherwise.`]},master:{heading:`Master revision table - Shoulder pain and frozen shoulder`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Pain from glenohumeral, AC, SC or scapulothoracic joints, cuff or bursa, or **referred** (neck, heart, lung apex, diaphragm); **adhesive capsulitis** = global active and passive restriction, **external rotation worst**, normal X-ray.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Rotator cuff related shoulder pain 60-70%** (replaces 'impingement'), frozen shoulder about 10%, AC joint about 10%, GH OA/instability, referred; frozen shoulder phases **freezing 2-9, frozen 4-12, thawing 5-24 months**.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Cuff: age, overhead work, scapular dyskinesis; frozen shoulder: **age 40-60, diabetes (10-20%)**, thyroid disease, immobilisation, stroke, Dupuytren.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Cuff: overload -> **tendinosis** (disorganised collagen, neovessels); frozen: synovitis -> **myofibroblast fibrosis** -> thick coracohumeral ligament -> **capsular volume under 10 mL** -> loss of ER.`],[`Key history`,`Age, dominant arm, night pain on lying on it, overhead pain, trauma (traumatic tear), diabetes, neck symptoms; **bilateral girdle pain over 50 with headache/jaw claudication - PMR/GCA**.`],[`Key examination`,`**Passive external rotation** (lost = capsulitis or OA), painful arc 60-120 degrees (cuff), last 30 degrees (AC); Jobe, ER lag, lift-off/belly-press; drop-arm; examine the neck.`],[`Investigations`,`X-ray AP + axillary if passive ER lost (exclude OA, posterior dislocation, calcific deposits); **ultrasound** for cuff tears; HbA1c in frozen shoulder; **ESR/CRP if bilateral girdle pain**.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Cuff: **12-week progressive loading programme**, paracetamol, **ibuprofen 400 mg TDS / naproxen 250-500 mg BD 1-2 weeks**; frozen: pain relief and gentle stretching within pain.`],[`Devices / procedures (injections)`,`Frozen shoulder (freezing phase): **IA triamcinolone or methylprednisolone 40 mg + 4-5 mL 1% lignocaine** (posterior approach); cuff: subacromial 40 mg to enable exercise; **max 3/year, 6 weeks apart**.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`**Hydrodilatation** (20-40 mL saline + steroid) at 3-6 months; suprascapular nerve block; **MUA or capsular release** after 6-9 months; cuff surgery if no progress at 3-6 months.`],[`Special groups`,`**Diabetics** - frozen shoulder more frequent, bilateral, resistant; check glucose 3-7 days after injection; **PMR - prednisolone 15 mg** (12.5-25), slow taper over 1-2 years with bone protection.`],[`Complications`,`Persistent stiffness, cuff tear progression and arthropathy, post-injection flare, hyperglycaemia, MUA fracture in osteoporotic bone.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`**Acute traumatic cuff tear under 60 - refer within 2 weeks**; unreduced dislocation, hot shoulder with fever, Pancoast or malignancy, neurological deficit, **PMR with GCA symptoms - prednisolone 40-60 mg now**.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Review **4-6 weeks**; measure **passive ER in degrees** and function (comb hair, bra tuck, reach shelf).`],[`Latest guideline change`,`No change in this pass: BESS/BOA 2020-2021 pathways and **BSR 2024 PMR guideline** remain current.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`**Loss of passive ER with normal X-ray = frozen shoulder**; impingement is now cuff-related shoulder pain; steroid works in the freezing phase; never miss bilateral girdle pain over 50.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Shoulder pain: one passive test sorts it`,steps:[{label:`Red flags? trauma, fever, mass, neuro, GCA`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Neck movements reproduce arm pain?`,detail:`Referred cervical pain`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Passive external rotation preserved?`,tone:`decision`},{label:`No - X-ray: normal = frozen shoulder`,detail:`Abnormal = GH OA, posterior dislocation`},{label:`Yes - painful arc, resisted cuff pain`,detail:`Rotator cuff related shoulder pain; weakness = consider tear`},{label:`Loading programme +/- injection`,detail:`Review 4-6 weeks`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Frozen shoulder by phase`,steps:[{label:`Freezing (painful) 2-9 months`,detail:`Analgesia, IA steroid 40 mg + lignocaine, gentle range exercise`},{label:`Frozen (stiff) 4-12 months`,detail:`Stretching within pain, home programme`},{label:`No progress at 3-6 months`,detail:`Hydrodilatation 20-40 mL; suprascapular nerve block`},{label:`Disabling after 6-9 months`,detail:`MUA or arthroscopic capsular release`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Thawing 5-24 months`,detail:`Self-limiting over 18-30 months total`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Cuff pain vs frozen shoulder vs PMR`,columns:[`Feature`,`Cuff related`,`Frozen shoulder`,`PMR`],rows:[[`Age`,`Any, 40-60 commonest`,`40-60, diabetics`,`Over 50`],[`Passive ER`,`Preserved`,`Lost (key sign)`,`Preserved but painful`],[`Laterality`,`Usually one`,`Usually one`,`Bilateral with hips`],[`Bloods`,`Normal`,`Normal (check HbA1c)`,`ESR/CRP raised`],[`Treatment`,`Loading, subacromial steroid`,`IA steroid, hydrodilatation`,`Prednisolone 15 mg`]]}]},"musculoskeletal-neck-pain":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Cervical spondylosis** is age-related degeneration of discs, facet and uncovertebral joints.`,`It is seen on X-ray in **over 90% above 70**, so it is never a diagnosis by itself.`,`Annual prevalence of neck pain is **30-50%** in adults.`,`Mechanical neck pain usually settles in **4-8 weeks**, with frequent recurrence.`,`**75-90% of radiculopathy** improves without surgery within 3 months.`,`**Myelopathy does not recover on its own**; it worsens stepwise.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Box 1: **mechanical (axial) neck pain** without neurology.`,`Box 2: **cervical radiculopathy**, dermatomal arm pain with segmental signs.`,`Box 3: **cervical myelopathy**, a progressive upper motor neurone syndrome.`,`Box 4: serious secondary causes: fracture, infection, tumour, inflammatory, vascular.`,`Myelopathy severity by **mJOA**: mild 15-17, moderate 12-14, severe under 12.`,`Whiplash by **Quebec grades 0-IV**; grades I-II are managed in primary care.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Each cervical root exits **above** its vertebra, so a **C5-C6 disc hits C6**.`,`C5-C6 and C6-C7 are the commonest levels because they move most.`,`The disc loses water and height, and load shifts to facet and uncovertebral joints.`,`**Osteophytes** narrow the foramen (root) and the canal (cord).`,`Disc chemicals (**TNF-alpha, phospholipase A2**) sensitise the root, adding chemical pain.`,`Cord compression causes ischaemia, giving **LMN signs at the level and UMN signs below**.`,`Prolonged compression kills neurones, so **early decompression** preserves function.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Pain **below the elbow with tingling** is radicular; ask which fingers tingle.`,`Ask the myelopathy questions: **handwriting change**, dropping things, buttons.`,`Also ask about **unsteady walking** and bladder urgency.`,`**Lhermitte phenomenon**: electric shock down the spine on neck flexion.`,`Red flags: fever, weight loss, past cancer, TB contact, constant night pain.`,`Sudden occipital pain with dizziness or diplopia suggests **vertebral artery dissection**.`,`Ask screen time, laptop height and **two-wheeler** riding for ergonomic advice.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Feel for **midline bony tenderness**, which suggests fracture, infection or tumour.`,`**Spurling test**: extend, side-bend and rotate to the painful side, press gently.`,`Arm pain relieved by **hand on head** supports root compression.`,`**Hoffmann sign** and an inverted supinator jerk point to the cord.`,`**Always examine the legs**: tone, clonus, plantars and tandem gait.`,`Exclude mimics: **carpal tunnel** (mimics C6), ulnar neuropathy (mimics C8), shoulder disease.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**No imaging** for mechanical neck pain without red flags or neurology.`,`After trauma, apply the **Canadian C-spine rule**; CT is first line in adults.`,`**MRI** for radiculopathy beyond 6 weeks or with progressive weakness.`,`**Urgent MRI** for any suspected myelopathy, infection or tumour.`,`ESR, CRP and CBC when infection, cancer or inflammatory disease is possible.`,`Spinal TB: MRI and **tissue for CBNAAT** before starting ATT.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Reassure**: degeneration is like grey hair; hurt does not equal harm.`,`**Neck-specific exercise**: chin tucks held 10 seconds, 10 repetitions, 3 times daily.`,`**Screen top at eye level**, laptop on a stand, break every 30-40 minutes.`,`Use one medium pillow; avoid sleeping on the stomach.`,`**Ibuprofen 400 mg TDS** or **naproxen 250-500 mg BD** for 5-7 days.`,`**No soft collar** beyond a few days and no high-velocity manipulation.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Acute spasm: **diazepam 2 mg TDS** for up to 5 days only.`,`Radicular pain: **amitriptyline 10 mg** at night, rising to 25-50 mg.`,`Alternatives: **duloxetine, pregabalin or gabapentin**; review at 4-8 weeks.`,`If one neuropathic drug fails, **switch class**.`,`Tramadol only briefly for severe acute radicular pain; **no long-term opioids**.`,`Epidural steroid is a specialist option for persistent radicular pain.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`**Clumsy hands, unsteady gait, brisk reflexes** mean myelopathy: urgent MRI.`,`New bladder or bowel dysfunction means **cord compression**: same day.`,`Fever with torticollis suggests TB or pyogenic spondylodiscitis.`,`RA with new neck pain and neurology suggests **atlantoaxial subluxation**.`,`Neck stiffness with fever and photophobia is **meningitis**.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review mechanical pain at **2-4 weeks** if not improving.`,`In radiculopathy, **re-examine power and reflexes** at every visit.`,`Refer radicular pain persisting beyond **4-6 weeks** for MRI and spinal opinion.`,`Myelopathy with **mJOA under 15** needs decompression.`,`Warn the anaesthetist about **rheumatoid necks** and myelopathy.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for neck pain`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Ibuprofen / naproxen`,`400 mg TDS / 250-500 mg BD oral`,`5-7 days, up to 2 weeks`,`GI bleed, AKI; PPI if over 45`],[`Paracetamol`,`500 mg-1 g up to QID oral`,`Short term`,`Max 3 g/day in elderly`],[`Diazepam`,`2 mg oral up to TDS`,`Maximum 5 days`,`Drowsiness, falls; no driving`],[`Amitriptyline`,`10 mg at night, up to 25-50 mg`,`Review at 4-8 weeks`,`Dry mouth, retention; avoid glaucoma`],[`Duloxetine`,`30 mg daily for 1 week, then 60 mg`,`Review at 4-8 weeks`,`Nausea, BP rise; taper to stop`],[`Pregabalin`,`75 mg BD, up to 150 mg BD`,`Review at 4-8 weeks`,`Dizziness, misuse; reduce in CKD`],[`Gabapentin`,`300 mg day 1, titrate to 1.8-3.6 g/day`,`Review at 4-8 weeks`,`Sedation, ataxia; reduce in CKD`],[`Tramadol`,`50 mg oral up to QID`,`Few days only`,`Seizures, serotonin syndrome`]]},{heading:`Mechanical vs radiculopathy vs myelopathy`,columns:[`Feature`,`Mechanical`,`Radiculopathy`,`Myelopathy`],rows:[[`Main complaint`,`Neck and trapezius ache`,`Arm pain below elbow, tingling`,`Clumsy hands, unsteady gait`],[`Signs`,`None`,`LMN weakness, reduced reflex at one root`,`Brisk reflexes, clonus, Hoffmann`],[`Plantars`,`Flexor`,`Flexor`,`Extensor`],[`Imaging`,`None`,`MRI if over 6 weeks or weak`,`Urgent MRI`],[`Plan`,`Exercise, ergonomics, NSAID`,`Neuropathic drugs, wait 6-12 weeks`,`Spinal surgery referral`]]},{heading:`Cervical root levels`,columns:[`Root (disc)`,`Sensory loss`,`Weakness`,`Reflex`],rows:[[`C5 (C4-C5)`,`Lateral shoulder (badge area)`,`Shoulder abduction`,`Biceps`],[`C6 (C5-C6)`,`Thumb and index finger`,`Elbow flexion, wrist extension`,`Supinator`],[`C7 (C6-C7)`,`Middle finger`,`Elbow extension (triceps)`,`Triceps`],[`C8 (C7-T1)`,`Ring and little finger`,`Finger flexion, grip`,`None reliable`],[`T1 (T1-T2)`,`Medial arm`,`Finger abduction`,`None; may give Horner`]]}],remember:[`Three boxes: mechanical, radiculopathy, myelopathy; plus red flags.`,`Myelopathy is found by examining the legs.`,`Handwriting change and dropping things mean myelopathy, not ageing.`,`C5-C6 disc hits C6: thumb numbness, lost supinator jerk.`,`Spurling is specific; hand on head relief supports root compression.`,`mJOA: 15-17 mild, 12-14 moderate, under 12 severe.`,`No collar, no manipulation: chin tucks and ergonomics instead.`]},master:{heading:`Master revision table - Neck pain and cervical spondylosis`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Cervical spondylosis** = age-related disc, facet and uncovertebral degeneration - present in over 70% at 60, so a radiological description, **never a diagnosis alone**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Mechanical (axial)**, **radiculopathy** (root), **myelopathy** (cord) + serious secondary causes; DCM severity by **mJOA: mild 15-17, moderate 12-14, severe under 12**; whiplash by Quebec grades 0-IV.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Age, posture (screens, two-wheelers, head-load carrying), occupation, trauma; RA (atlantoaxial subluxation), AS, TB, metastasis.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Disc height loss -> uncovertebral and facet osteophytes -> **foraminal narrowing (root)** or **canal narrowing + ligamentum flavum buckling (cord)** -> dynamic cord injury -> myelopathy.`],[`Key history`,`Arm pain in dermatome, paraesthesia; **myelopathy questions: handwriting change, dropping things, buttons, unsteady gait, bladder urgency**; trauma mechanism; fever, weight loss, RA.`],[`Key examination`,`Neck ROM, **Spurling** (specific), shoulder abduction relief; root-wise power/reflexes; **legs - hyperreflexia, clonus, Babinski, Hoffmann, tandem gait**.`],[`Investigations`,`**No imaging for mechanical pain**; after trauma apply **Canadian C-spine rule** (CT first-line in adults); **MRI for radiculopathy over 6 weeks or progressive weakness, urgently for myelopathy**.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Reassure, stay active, **neck-specific exercise**, ergonomics; **ibuprofen 400 mg TDS / naproxen 250-500 mg BD 5-7 days** with PPI if at risk; paracetamol as adjunct.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Spasm: **diazepam 2 mg TDS up to 5 days** or tizanidine; radicular: **amitriptyline 10 mg -> 25-50 mg**, duloxetine 30 -> 60 mg or pregabalin 75 mg BD -> 150 mg BD; short tramadol.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Specialist epidural steroid for persistent radicular pain; **no high-velocity manipulation** (vertebral dissection); decompression for moderate-severe myelopathy.`],[`Special groups`,`**RA** - atlantoaxial subluxation, never Spurling or manipulate, warn anaesthetist; elderly - avoid diazepam; CKD - reduce gabapentinoids.`],[`Complications`,`Chronic pain, progressive **myelopathy (does not recover spontaneously)**, cord injury after minor trauma, opioid/benzodiazepine dependence.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Myelopathy signs - **urgent MRI and spinal referral**; trauma with midline tenderness; fever, raised CRP (infection, TB); cancer; new bladder dysfunction; progressive weakness.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Review 2-6 weeks; radiculopathy settles in **75-90% within 3 months**; mild DCM - surveillance every 3-6 months with mJOA.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`No change in this pass: NICE CKS neck pain, AO Spine DCM guideline and NICE NG41 (C-spine imaging) remain current.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`**Myelopathy is found by examining the legs**; clumsy hands are myelopathy, not age; mJOA under 15 goes to surgery; Spurling specific, not sensitive.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Neck pain: sort into three boxes`,steps:[{label:`Trauma? apply Canadian C-spine rule`,detail:`High risk: 65+, dangerous mechanism, paraesthesia - CT`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Fever, weight loss, cancer, RA?`,detail:`Infection, tumour, atlantoaxial instability - urgent MRI`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Myelopathy signs in hands or legs?`,detail:`Hoffmann, clonus, Babinski, tandem gait`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Yes - urgent MRI, spinal surgeon`,detail:`mJOA under 15 - decompression`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Dermatomal arm pain - radiculopathy`,detail:`NSAID, neuropathic agent; MRI if over 6 weeks or weakness`},{label:`Mechanical - exercise and ergonomics`,detail:`Settles in 4-8 weeks`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Mechanical vs radiculopathy vs myelopathy`,columns:[`Feature`,`Mechanical`,`Radiculopathy`,`Myelopathy`],rows:[[`Main symptom`,`Neck pain, stiffness`,`Dermatomal arm pain`,`Clumsy hands, unsteady gait`],[`Signs`,`Tenderness only`,`LMN: weak, reflex lost at level`,`UMN: hyperreflexia, Hoffmann, Babinski`],[`Test`,`None`,`Spurling positive`,`Tandem gait, Romberg`],[`Imaging`,`None`,`MRI if over 6 weeks or weakness`,`Urgent MRI`],[`Course`,`Settles 4-8 weeks`,`75-90% settle in 3 months`,`Progressive - refer`]]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Cervical radiculopathy: treatment ladder`,steps:[{label:`Reassure, keep moving, neck exercise`,detail:`75-90% settle in 3 months; no collar, no manipulation`,tone:`good`},{label:`Short NSAID course`,detail:`Ibuprofen 400 mg TDS or naproxen 250-500 mg BD, 5-7 days; PPI if at risk`},{label:`Neuropathic agent for arm pain`,detail:`Amitriptyline 10 mg nocte to 25-50 mg, duloxetine 30-60 mg or pregabalin 75-150 mg BD`},{label:`Review 4-8 weeks; switch class if no benefit`,detail:`Re-examine power, reflexes and look for myelopathy`,tone:`decision`},{label:`MRI and spinal opinion`,detail:`Pain beyond 4-6 weeks despite treatment or progressive weakness`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Epidural steroid or ACDF/foraminotomy`,detail:`Specialist; surgery for persistent pain or deficit`}]}]},"musculoskeletal-osteoporosis":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Osteoporosis** is low bone mass with damaged microarchitecture, causing fragility fractures.`,`Bone present is **normally mineralised**, unlike osteomalacia.`,`Diagnosis: **DXA T-score -2.5 or below**, or any hip or vertebral fragility fracture.`,`A **fragility fracture** follows a fall from standing height or less.`,`In India, hip fractures occur **10-20 years earlier** than in the West.`,`One-year mortality after hip fracture is **20-30%**.`,`**Vitamin D deficiency** (25(OH)D under 20 ng/mL) affects 70-90% of Indian adults.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`WHO T-score: normal -1.0 or above, **osteopenia -1.0 to -2.5**, osteoporosis -2.5 or below.`,`**Severe osteoporosis** is -2.5 or below plus a fragility fracture.`,`Use **Z-score** in premenopausal women and men under 50; -2.0 or below is abnormal.`,`**Primary**: postmenopausal (type I, vertebra and wrist) and age-related (type II, hip).`,`**Secondary**: glucocorticoids, other drugs and diseases.`,`Vertebral fracture (Genant): grade 1 is 20-25%, grade 2 is 25-40%, grade 3 over 40% height loss.`]},{title:`Causes and risk factors`,points:[`**Early menopause** (before 45), amenorrhoea, low BMI under 19.`,`**Prednisolone 2.5-5 mg or more** daily for 3 months or longer.`,`Aromatase inhibitors, antiepileptics, excess thyroxine, long-term PPI, heparin.`,`Parental hip fracture, smoking, alcohol over 3 units a day, inactivity.`,`Low dietary calcium: Indians take only **300-600 mg/day** against a need of 1000-1200 mg.`,`Falls: sedatives, postural hypotension, poor vision and sarcopenia.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Osteoclasts resorb bone and osteoblasts refill it; osteoporosis is a **negative balance**.`,`**RANKL** drives osteoclasts; osteoprotegerin brakes them; **denosumab blocks RANKL**.`,`Oestrogen loss raises osteoclast activity, causing **2-3% trabecular loss a year** for 5-10 years.`,`Peak bone mass is reached by **25-30 years**; later loss is subtracted from it.`,`Steroids kill osteoblasts; loss is fastest in the **first 6-12 months**.`,`Thin intact bone is painless; **pain means fracture** or another diagnosis.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask **previous fractures** and their mechanism; a fragility fracture predicts the next.`,`**Historical height loss over 4 cm** suggests vertebral fractures.`,`Ask steroid use, including **'desi' medicines** that may contain steroids.`,`Ask **falls in the last year**, dizziness on standing, vision and home hazards.`,`Diffuse bone pain with proximal weakness suggests **osteomalacia**.`,`Before a bisphosphonate, ask **dysphagia, reflux, kidney disease and dental health**.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`**Measure height** every visit; measured loss over 2 cm suggests a new fracture.`,`**Occiput-to-wall gap** suggests thoracic fractures; rib-to-pelvis under 2 fingers suggests lumbar.`,`**Timed Up and Go 12 seconds or more** flags falls risk.`,`Check **postural BP**, vision, balance and footwear.`,`Look for Cushingoid habitus, thyroid swelling and hypogonadism in men.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**DXA of hip and lumbar spine** is the diagnostic test.`,`In older Indians, trust the **femoral neck**; spondylosis falsely raises spine BMD.`,`**FRAX (Indian model)** gives 10-year hip and major fracture probability.`,`Baseline: **calcium, phosphate, ALP, creatinine, 25(OH)D**, CBC, ESR, LFT, TSH.`,`Pure osteoporosis has **normal calcium, phosphate and ALP**.`,`Electrophoresis and light chains if fractures come with anaemia or high ESR.`,`**Lateral spine X-ray** for height loss or kyphosis finds silent vertebral fractures.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Aim is **fracture prevention**, not a normal T-score.`,`**Cholecalciferol 60,000 IU weekly for 8 weeks**, then monthly.`,`Reach calcium **1000-1200 mg/day**, diet first: milk, curd, ragi, paneer, til.`,`**Alendronate 70 mg once weekly** for 5 years, then reassess.`,`Take it on waking, empty stomach, **full glass of plain water**, upright 30 minutes.`,`**Falls prevention**: strength and balance exercise, review sedatives, grab bars, night lights.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**Zoledronic acid 5 mg IV yearly** for intolerance, poor adherence or after hip fracture.`,`Check creatinine and replete vitamin D before zoledronic acid.`,`**Denosumab 60 mg SC every 6 months**, useful in CKD.`,`**Never stop or delay denosumab** beyond 7 months without a bisphosphonate.`,`**Teriparatide 20 micrograms SC daily**, maximum 24 months, for very high risk.`,`Raloxifene 60 mg daily reduces vertebral fractures but **not hip fractures**.`,`**Glucocorticoid users** need bone protection from the start if steroids last 3 months or more.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review at **3 months**, then yearly for adherence, height, falls and side effects.`,`Repeat DXA after **2-3 years** on the same machine.`,`Drug holiday after **5 years oral or 3 years IV** only if low risk.`,`New **thigh or groin pain** on a bisphosphonate: X-ray both femora.`,`Every fragility fracture goes to a **fracture liaison service**.`,`Refer men, premenopausal women, **Z-score -2.0 or less** and fracture on treatment.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for osteoporosis`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Cholecalciferol`,`60,000 IU oral weekly, then monthly`,`8 weeks loading, then maintenance`,`Hypercalcaemia with megadoses`],[`Calcium carbonate / citrate`,`Elemental 500 mg oral once or twice daily`,`Long term, for the shortfall`,`Constipation; 2 h apart from bisphosphonate`],[`Alendronate`,`70 mg oral once weekly`,`5 years, then reassess`,`Oesophagitis; avoid eGFR under 35`],[`Risedronate`,`35 mg oral once weekly`,`5 years, then reassess`,`As alendronate; avoid eGFR under 30`],[`Zoledronic acid`,`5 mg IV over 15 min yearly`,`Usually 3 doses`,`Flu-like reaction, hypocalcaemia, renal toxicity`],[`Denosumab`,`60 mg SC every 6 months`,`Long term; never stop abruptly`,`Hypocalcaemia; rebound vertebral fractures`],[`Teriparatide`,`20 micrograms SC daily`,`Maximum 24 months`,`Hypercalcaemia; follow with antiresorptive`],[`Raloxifene`,`60 mg oral once daily`,`Long term`,`VTE, hot flushes; no hip benefit`]]},{heading:`WHO T-score classification`,columns:[`Category`,`T-score`,`Action`],rows:[[`Normal`,`-1.0 or above`,`Lifestyle, calcium and vitamin D`],[`Osteopenia`,`Between -1.0 and -2.5`,`Calculate FRAX; treat if high risk`],[`Osteoporosis`,`-2.5 or below`,`Replete vitamin D, then bisphosphonate`],[`Severe osteoporosis`,`-2.5 or below plus fracture`,`Treat; consider anabolic first`],[`Z-score -2.0 or below`,`Below expected for age`,`Search for a secondary cause`]]},{heading:`Osteoporosis vs osteomalacia`,columns:[`Feature`,`Osteoporosis`,`Osteomalacia`],rows:[[`Defect`,`Low bone mass, normal mineral`,`Poorly mineralised osteoid`],[`Symptoms`,`Silent until fracture`,`Bone pain, proximal myopathy, waddling gait`],[`Calcium and phosphate`,`Normal`,`Low or low-normal`],[`ALP and PTH`,`Normal`,`Raised`],[`X-ray`,`Fractures, wedged vertebrae`,`Looser zones`],[`Treatment`,`Vitamin D, calcium, antiresorptive`,`Vitamin D loading; no bisphosphonate yet`]]}],remember:[`A hip or vertebral fragility fracture is osteoporosis whatever the DXA says.`,`T-score: -1 and -2.5 are the cut-offs; Z-score -2.0 in the young.`,`Replete vitamin D before the first bisphosphonate.`,`Alendronate: empty stomach, full glass of water, upright 30 minutes.`,`Trust the femoral neck in the elderly.`,`Never stop denosumab without a follow-on bisphosphonate.`,`High ALP: fracture, osteomalacia, Paget or metastasis.`]},updated:[`NOGG 2024 (minor update of 2021, published 2025) replaces NOGG 2022 as the UK reference; FRAX thresholds are age-dependent with a very-high-risk category - the fixed hip 3%/major 20% cut-offs are US (BHOF).`,`NICE NG259 (July 2026) replaces CG146: assess everyone 50+ and postmenopausal women with a fragility fracture or on systemic steroids; consider women 65+, men 75+; treatment without DXA allowed in frailty.`,`Endocrine Society 2024: no routine 25(OH)D testing or fixed sufficiency target in healthy adults; empiric vitamin D only for children, over 75s, pregnancy and high-risk prediabetes.`,`NICE NG249 (2025) replaces CG161 for falls; World Falls Guidelines 2022 use gait speed under 0.8 m/s or TUG over 15 s; strength and balance exercise is the core intervention.`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Osteoporosis and vitamin D deficiency`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Low bone mass + microarchitectural deterioration -> fragility; **DXA T-score -2.5 or below** or **any hip or vertebral fragility fracture** (fall from standing height or less).`],[`Relevant classification`,`WHO T-score: **normal -1.0 or above, osteopenia -1.0 to -2.5, osteoporosis -2.5 or below, severe = plus fracture**; **Z-score -2.0 or below** (premenopausal, men under 50) = secondary cause search; Genant vertebral grades 1-3.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Age, female, **menopause before 45**, low BMI (under 19), parental hip fracture, smoking, alcohol over 3 units/day, **glucocorticoids**, aromatase inhibitors, ADT, antiepileptics, RA, hypogonadism, vitamin D deficiency.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Oestrogen loss -> **RANKL up, OPG down** -> osteoclast excess -> trabecular loss 2-3%/year -> thin perforated trabeculae -> **fracture**; painless until it breaks.`],[`Key history`,`Previous fractures and mechanism, **height loss over 4 cm**, back pain, menopause, steroids, dietary calcium (Indian intake 300-600 mg), falls in last year, dysphagia/dental work before bisphosphonate.`],[`Key examination`,`Measured height (**loss over 2 cm**), kyphosis, **occiput-to-wall over 5 cm**, rib-to-pelvis under 2 fingers; TUG, postural BP, vision; Cushingoid, hypogonadal, osteomalacia signs.`],[`Investigations`,`**DXA hip and spine** (trust femoral neck in elderly), **FRAX (Indian)**, lateral spine X-ray/VFA; Ca, PO4, ALP, creatinine, 25(OH)D, TSH, CBC, ESR; SPEP and light chains if myeloma possible.`],[`Diagnostic criteria - who to assess (NICE NG259 2026)`,`**Everyone 50+ and postmenopausal women with fragility fracture or on systemic steroids**; consider **women 65+, men 75+** and 50-74 with risk factors; reassess untreated low-risk after 5 years.`],[`Treatment - general`,`**Calcium 1000-1200 mg/day from diet** (milk, ragi, curd), protein 1-1.2 g/kg, sunlight, weight-bearing + resistance + **balance exercise**, stop smoking, falls prevention (home modification, drug review).`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Replete vitamin D: **cholecalciferol 60,000 IU weekly x 8 weeks, then monthly**; then **alendronate 70 mg once weekly** - fasting, full glass of plain water, **upright and nil else 30 min**; 5 years then reassess.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`**Zoledronic acid 5 mg IV yearly** (oral intolerance, after hip fracture, eGFR 35+); **denosumab 60 mg SC 6-monthly** (CKD; never stop without bisphosphonate); **teriparatide 20 mcg SC daily up to 24 months** or romosozumab for very high risk.`],[`Special groups`,`**GIOP** - bone protection from start if steroids 3 months+; **CKD** - avoid bisphosphonate under eGFR 30-35, denosumab with Ca checks, exclude CKD-MBD; men - testosterone only if hypogonadal; MHT in women under 60.`],[`Vitamin D (2024 position)`,`ISBMR: deficiency **under 20 ng/mL**; Endocrine Society 2024 - **no routine testing in healthy adults**, empiric D for children, **over 75s, pregnancy, prediabetes**; test and treat in osteoporosis and osteomalacia.`],[`Complications`,`Hip fracture (**1-year mortality 20-30%**), vertebral collapse and kyphosis; drug - oesophagitis, acute-phase reaction, hypocalcaemia, **ONJ and atypical femoral fracture** (rare), **denosumab rebound fractures**.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Back pain + neurology - MRI; fractures with anaemia/high ESR - myeloma; thigh pain on bisphosphonate - X-ray both femora; Z-score -2.0 or less, men, fracture on treatment - specialist.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Review 3 months then yearly (adherence under 50% at 1 year); DXA every 2-3 years same machine; **drug holiday after 5 years oral / 3 years IV** if low risk; no holiday for denosumab.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**NICE NG259 2026** replaces CG146; **NOGG 2024** minor update; **Endocrine Society 2024** vitamin D; **NICE NG249 2025** falls replaces CG161.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Hip/vertebral fragility fracture = osteoporosis whatever the DXA; **replete vitamin D before zoledronic acid**; never stop denosumab abruptly; teriparatide must be followed by an antiresorptive.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Fragility fracture or risk factor: assessment pathway`,steps:[{label:`Who to assess (NICE NG259)`,detail:`50+ or postmenopausal with fracture or steroids; women 65+, men 75+`},{label:`Hip or vertebral fragility fracture?`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Yes - treat; DXA not needed to start`,detail:`Fracture liaison; exclude myeloma, metastasis`,tone:`warn`},{label:`FRAX (Indian) +/- DXA femoral neck`,detail:`NOGG age-dependent thresholds; US hip 3%, major 20%`},{label:`Bloods and vitamin D status`,detail:`Ca, ALP, creatinine, 25(OH)D, TSH; SPEP if indicated`},{label:`High risk: replete D, start alendronate`,detail:`Very high risk: refer for anabolic first`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Osteoporosis drug ladder`,steps:[{label:`Vitamin D and calcium first`,detail:`Cholecalciferol 60,000 IU weekly x 8 then monthly; calcium only for shortfall`},{label:`Alendronate 70 mg weekly`,detail:`Fasting, 200 mL water, upright 30 min; avoid eGFR under 35; 5 years`},{label:`Zoledronic acid 5 mg IV yearly`,detail:`Over 15 min; hydrate; paracetamol 3 days; usually 3 doses`},{label:`Denosumab 60 mg SC 6-monthly`,detail:`CKD option; never delay beyond 7 months; follow with bisphosphonate`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Teriparatide 20 mcg daily up to 24 months`,detail:`Very high risk; then antiresorptive`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Osteoporosis vs osteomalacia`,columns:[`Feature`,`Osteoporosis`,`Osteomalacia`],rows:[[`Defect`,`Low mass, normal mineral`,`Unmineralised osteoid`],[`Symptoms`,`Silent until fracture`,`Bone pain, proximal myopathy`],[`Ca, PO4, ALP, PTH`,`Normal`,`Low Ca/PO4, high ALP and PTH`],[`X-ray`,`Wedged vertebrae`,`Looser zones`],[`Treatment`,`D, Ca + antiresorptive`,`D and Ca loading; no bisphosphonate first`]]}]},"musculoskeletal-spondyloarthropathy-sle":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Spondyloarthritis (SpA)** is a family of RF-negative, **HLA-B27-linked** inflammatory diseases.`,`Members: **axial SpA (ankylosing spondylitis)**, psoriatic, reactive and enteropathic arthritis.`,`HLA-B27 is carried by **6-8% of Indians**, but AS affects only 0.2-0.5%.`,`**SLE** is a multisystem immune-complex disease with antinuclear antibodies.`,`SLE affects women **9:1**, usually aged **15-45**.`,`**Lupus nephritis** affects 40-60% of Indian patients.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**ASAS axial SpA**: back pain 3 months or more, onset **before 45**.`,`Imaging arm: **sacroiliitis plus 1 SpA feature**.`,`Clinical arm: **HLA-B27 plus 2 other SpA features**.`,`**Inflammatory back pain**: 4 of 5 ASAS features (onset under 40, insidious, exercise helps, rest does not, night pain).`,`Psoriatic arthritis is classified by **CASPAR**, 3 or more points.`,`SLE (EULAR/ACR 2019): entry **ANA 1:80 or more**, then **10 or more points** with one clinical item.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`**Enthesitis** is the core lesion of SpA; synovitis is the core lesion of RA.`,`The **IL-23/IL-17 axis** and TNF drive SpA inflammation.`,`Sacroiliitis goes from marrow oedema to erosions, sclerosis and finally **ankylosis**.`,`Syndesmophytes bridge vertebrae, giving the **bamboo spine**.`,`In SLE, poor clearance of dying cells exposes nuclear antigens.`,`Immune complexes deposit in kidney, skin and serosa and **consume C3 and C4**.`,`Interferon suppresses CRP, so **CRP stays normal** in a lupus flare.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Back pain waking the patient in the **second half of the night**, better with exercise.`,`**Alternating buttock pain** that shifts from side to side means sacroiliitis.`,`Ask about **heel pain, a sausage digit** and asymmetric knee or ankle swelling.`,`Ask **painful red eye with photophobia**: acute anterior uveitis.`,`Ask psoriasis, bloody diarrhoea, and dysentery or urethritis 1-4 weeks earlier.`,`SLE screen: **photosensitive rash, mouth ulcers, hair fall, Raynaud**, pleuritic pain, fever.`,`Ask **recurrent miscarriage and thrombosis** (antiphospholipid syndrome).`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`**Modified Schober**: increase under 5 cm means restricted lumbar flexion.`,`**Chest expansion** under 5 cm and any occiput-to-wall gap are abnormal.`,`**FABER test** and pressure on entheses such as the Achilles insertion.`,`Look for **nail pitting**, hidden psoriasis and an early diastolic murmur.`,`SLE: **malar rash sparing nasolabial folds**, painless palatal ulcers.`,`**Jaccoud arthropathy**: deformities that correct passively, unlike RA.`,`Check **BP and urine dipstick** at every SLE visit.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`SpA: CBC, **ESR and CRP**; these are normal in up to 40% of axial disease.`,`**HLA-B27** only when suspicion exists; it supports, never screens.`,`**X-ray pelvis** first; **MRI SI joints (STIR)** if the film is normal.`,`SLE screen: **ANA on HEp-2 cells**, over 95% sensitive.`,`**Anti-dsDNA** tracks nephritis; **anti-Smith** is the most specific.`,`SLE organ screen: CBC, creatinine, **urine protein-creatinine ratio**, C3, C4.`,`Before biologics: **chest X-ray, IGRA or Mantoux, HBsAg**, anti-HCV, HIV.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`SpA: **daily exercise** (spinal extension, posture, breathing) is the cornerstone.`,`**Full-dose NSAID continuously** while active: naproxen 500 mg BD or etoricoxib 90 mg.`,`Try **two NSAIDs for 2-4 weeks each** before calling failure.`,`**Sulfasalazine or methotrexate** help peripheral arthritis only, not the spine.`,`SLE: **hydroxychloroquine for every patient, lifelong**, max 5 mg/kg.`,`SLE: **SPF 50 sunscreen**, stop smoking, control BP and lipids.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Axial SpA failing two NSAIDs: **TNF or IL-17 inhibitor**, equal first choices.`,`**Uveitis or IBD**: choose a monoclonal anti-TNF (adalimumab), not etanercept.`,`**Psoriasis**: choose an IL-17 inhibitor such as secukinumab.`,`Screen and treat **latent TB and hepatitis B** before any biologic or JAK inhibitor.`,`SLE flare: **prednisolone 0.5-1 mg/kg** for organ disease, taper to 5 mg or less.`,`Steroid-sparing drugs are **methotrexate, azathioprine or mycophenolate**.`,`**Lupus nephritis**: mycophenolate or cyclophosphamide with steroids, plus belimumab or CNI.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`SLE pregnancy: plan after **6 months of quiet disease**.`,`**Continue hydroxychloroquine** and add aspirin 75-150 mg from 12 weeks.`,`**Stop mycophenolate 6 weeks** and methotrexate 1-3 months before conception.`,`Anti-Ro mothers need fetal heart monitoring for **congenital heart block**.`,`APS in pregnancy: **aspirin plus LMWH**; avoid the combined oestrogen pill.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Refer suspected axial SpA: back pain over 3 months, onset before 45, inflammatory features.`,`SpA review every 3-6 months with **BASDAI or ASDAS**; biologic response at 12 weeks.`,`SLE review every 3-6 months: **BP, dipstick, creatinine, CBC, C3, C4, anti-dsDNA**.`,`Hydroxychloroquine **eye screening yearly after 5 years** (after 1 year if high risk).`,`Urgent: **uveitis (same day)**, new proteinuria, platelets under 50,000, seizures.`,`AS after minor trauma: suspect an **unstable spinal fracture**; get CT.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for spondyloarthritis and SLE`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Naproxen / etoricoxib (SpA)`,`500 mg BD / 90 mg OD oral`,`Continuous while active`,`GI bleed, AKI, BP; PPI if at risk`],[`Sulfasalazine`,`500 mg daily, up to 1 g BD`,`Long term, peripheral only`,`Orange urine, agranulocytosis, hepatitis`],[`Methotrexate`,`7.5-15 mg weekly, up to 20-25 mg`,`Long term; folic acid 5 mg weekly`,`Hepatotoxicity, cytopenia; teratogenic`],[`Adalimumab`,`40 mg SC every 2 weeks`,`Review at 12 weeks`,`TB reactivation, serious infection`],[`Secukinumab`,`150 mg SC weekly x 5, then monthly`,`Review at 12 weeks`,`Candidiasis; can worsen IBD`],[`Hydroxychloroquine`,`200-400 mg oral daily, max 5 mg/kg`,`Lifelong`,`Retinopathy; QT prolongation`],[`Prednisolone (SLE flare)`,`0.5-1 mg/kg/day oral`,`Taper to 5 mg/day or less`,`Infection, diabetes, osteoporosis`],[`Azathioprine`,`1-2 mg/kg/day oral`,`Long term`,`Marrow suppression; check TPMT; no allopurinol`],[`Mycophenolate mofetil`,`2-3 g/day oral in two doses`,`Induction and maintenance`,`Teratogenic; diarrhoea, cytopenia`]]},{heading:`SLE antibodies`,columns:[`Test`,`What it shows`,`When`],rows:[[`ANA (HEp-2)`,`Over 95% sensitive screen`,`Only with objective features`],[`Anti-dsDNA`,`Specific; tracks nephritis activity`,`If ANA positive; each review`],[`Anti-Smith`,`Most specific, 20-30% sensitive`,`If ANA positive; once`],[`Anti-Ro / La`,`Neonatal lupus, heart block`,`Before pregnancy`],[`Anti-histone`,`Drug-induced lupus`,`On hydralazine, isoniazid`],[`C3 and C4`,`Low in active disease`,`Diagnosis and each review`],[`Antiphospholipid antibodies`,`Thrombosis, pregnancy loss`,`Diagnosis, before pregnancy`]]},{heading:`Inflammatory vs mechanical back pain`,columns:[`Feature`,`Inflammatory (axial SpA)`,`Mechanical`],rows:[[`Age at onset`,`Under 40-45`,`Any age`],[`Onset`,`Insidious`,`Often after strain`],[`Morning stiffness`,`Over 30 minutes`,`Under 30 minutes`],[`Exercise`,`Improves`,`Worsens`],[`Rest`,`No improvement`,`Improves`],[`Night pain`,`Second half of night`,`Uncommon`],[`NSAID response`,`Dramatic within 48 hours`,`Partial`]]}],remember:[`Enthesitis is the core lesion of SpA; synovitis of RA.`,`HLA-B27 supports, never screens; CRP is normal in up to 40%.`,`AS bedside trio: Schober under 5 cm, chest expansion under 5 cm, occiput-to-wall gap.`,`Uveitis or IBD: monoclonal anti-TNF; psoriasis: IL-17 inhibitor.`,`Screen TB and hepatitis B before every biologic.`,`ANA screens; anti-dsDNA tracks nephritis; anti-Smith is most specific.`,`In SLE a high CRP means infection or serositis.`,`Every SLE patient takes hydroxychloroquine lifelong; dipstick and BP every visit.`]},updated:[`ACR/SAA/SPARTAN 2026: TNF and IL-17 inhibitors equal as first biologic for axial SpA, both preferred over JAK inhibitors; monoclonal anti-TNF if uveitis or IBD; switch mechanism after first b/tsDMARD failure.`,`ACR 2025 SLE: hydroxychloroquine for nearly all, continued indefinitely, target 5 mg/kg/day or less; minimise and stop glucocorticoids; early immunosuppressants; taper after 3-5 years of remission.`,`ACR 2024 lupus nephritis: triple therapy for class III/IV (steroid + MMF + belimumab, MMF + CNI, or low-dose CYC + belimumab); prednisone 0.5 mg/kg (max 40) to 5 mg or less by 6 months.`,`BSR 2025 csDMARD guideline replaces BSR 2017: bloods at week 2, monthly for 3-6 months, then 3-monthly (risk-stratified); HCQ eye screen yearly after 5 years, after 1 year if high risk.`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Spondyloarthritis and SLE`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**SpA**: HLA-B27-linked, seronegative, **enthesitis-led** family (axial SpA, PsA, reactive, enteropathic); **SLE**: ANA-positive immune-complex multisystem disease, women 9:1, age 15-45.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**ASAS 2009 axSpA**: back pain 3 months+, onset under 45 + sacroiliitis + 1 feature or HLA-B27 + 2 features; **CASPAR** 3+ for PsA; **EULAR/ACR 2019 SLE**: ANA 1:80+ entry, **10+ points with one clinical item**.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`HLA-B27 (6-8% of Indians), gut dysbiosis, enteric or chlamydial infection (reactive); SLE - genetics, UV light, oestrogen, drugs (hydralazine, isoniazid, procainamide).`],[`Pathophysiology`,`SpA: **IL-23 -> IL-17/TNF at entheses** -> erosion -> new bone (syndesmophytes, bamboo spine); SLE: poor apoptotic clearance -> **type I interferon** -> autoantibodies -> immune complexes -> **low C3/C4**.`],[`Key history`,`**Inflammatory back pain** (under 40, insidious, better with exercise, not rest, second-half-night pain), heel pain, dactylitis, red eye, psoriasis, IBD; SLE - rash, ulcers, alopecia, Raynaud, frothy urine, miscarriage.`],[`Key examination`,`**Modified Schober under 5 cm**, occiput-to-wall over 0, chest expansion under 5 cm, FABER, entheses; SLE - malar rash, palatal ulcers, Jaccoud hands, **BP and urine dipstick every visit**.`],[`Investigations`,`SpA: CRP (normal in 40%), **HLA-B27 only if suspected**, AP pelvis, **MRI SI joints (STIR)**; SLE: **ANA on HEp-2**, then anti-dsDNA, anti-Sm, C3/C4, CBC, urine PCR (over 0.5 g/g = biopsy), aPL.`],[`Treatment - SpA first line`,`**Exercise and posture daily** + **continuous full-dose NSAID** (naproxen 500 mg BD, etoricoxib 90 mg OD) - **two NSAIDs 2-4 weeks each** before failure; sulfasalazine/MTX for peripheral joints only.`],[`Treatment - SpA escalation`,`ASDAS 2.1+ despite 2 NSAIDs: **TNFi or IL-17i (equal first choice - ACR 2026)**; adalimumab 40 mg SC 2-weekly, secukinumab 150 mg weekly x5 then monthly; **uveitis/IBD - monoclonal anti-TNF**; JAKi later.`],[`Treatment - SLE first line`,`**Hydroxychloroquine 200-400 mg, 5 mg/kg or less, lifelong** for every patient; sun protection (SPF 50); **prednisolone lowest dose, taper to 5 mg or less and stop if possible**.`],[`Treatment - SLE escalation`,`Steroid-sparing early: **MTX, azathioprine 1-2 mg/kg, MMF 2-3 g/day**; belimumab, anifrolumab, rituximab; **LN class III/IV - triple therapy** (steroid + MMF + belimumab or CNI, or Euro-Lupus CYC + belimumab).`],[`Special groups`,`**SLE pregnancy** - plan after 6 months quiescence, continue HCQ, **aspirin 75-150 mg from 12 weeks**, stop MMF 6 weeks before; anti-Ro - fetal heart monitoring; APS - LMWH + aspirin; no live vaccines on biologics.`],[`Complications`,`SpA - **anterior uveitis**, aortic regurgitation, heart block, fused-spine fracture; SLE - **nephritis**, NPSLE, cytopenias, APS thrombosis, infection, premature CVD, steroid damage.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Red eye with photophobia - **same-day ophthalmology**; neck pain after minor trauma in AS - CT; proteinuria/rising creatinine - biopsy; fever on biologic - infection incl. TB; platelets under 50,000.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`SpA: **ASDAS** 3-6 monthly, biologic review at 12 weeks (ASDAS fall 1.1+); SLE: urine PCR, creatinine, CBC, C3/C4, dsDNA 3-6 monthly; **BSR 2025 bloods: week 2, monthly 3-6 months, then 3-monthly**.`],[`Prevention / programme (pre-biologic screen)`,`**Latent TB (IGRA/TST + CXR) with TPT (6H or 3HP) 1 month before**, HBsAg/anti-HBc, HCV, HIV; inactivated vaccines (influenza, pneumococcal, recombinant zoster).`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**ACR 2026 axSpA** (TNFi = IL-17i first), **ACR 2025 SLE** (universal HCQ, stop steroids, early immunosuppression), **ACR 2024 LN triple therapy**, **BSR 2025** csDMARD monitoring.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Enthesitis is the SpA lesion; HLA-B27 supports, never screens; **csDMARDs fail axially**; ANA screens, **anti-dsDNA tracks nephritis**, anti-Sm most specific; **high CRP in SLE = infection or serositis**.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Young adult with chronic back pain: is it axial SpA?`,steps:[{label:`Back pain over 3 months, onset under 45`},{label:`Inflammatory features 4 of 5?`,detail:`Insidious, under 40, better with exercise, not rest, night pain`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Ask uveitis, psoriasis, IBD, heel pain, family`,detail:`Any SpA feature raises probability`},{label:`CRP, HLA-B27, AP pelvis`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Pelvis normal but high suspicion - MRI SI STIR`,detail:`Bone marrow oedema = non-radiographic axSpA`},{label:`Refer rheumatology; start NSAID + exercise`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Axial SpA treatment ladder (ASAS-EULAR 2022 / ACR 2026)`,steps:[{label:`Exercise, posture, stop smoking`,detail:`Daily extension and breathing exercises, swimming`,tone:`good`},{label:`Continuous full-dose NSAID`,detail:`Naproxen 500 mg BD or etoricoxib 90 mg OD; two NSAIDs 2-4 weeks each`},{label:`Peripheral joints only: sulfasalazine`,detail:`1 g BD; no effect on axial disease`,tone:`warn`},{label:`TNFi or IL-17i (equal first biologic)`,detail:`ASDAS 2.1+; screen TB and HBV; anti-TNF mAb if uveitis/IBD`},{label:`Fail: switch mechanism; JAKi option`,detail:`Tofacitinib 5 mg BD or upadacitinib 15 mg OD after risk review`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`SLE treatment by severity (ACR 2025 / EULAR 2023)`,steps:[{label:`Hydroxychloroquine for all, lifelong`,detail:`5 mg/kg/day or less; eye screen yearly after 5 years`,tone:`good`},{label:`Mild: short low-dose steroid, NSAID`,detail:`Skin and joints; sun protection`},{label:`Moderate: early steroid-sparing agent`,detail:`MTX, azathioprine 1-2 mg/kg or MMF; taper prednisolone to 5 mg or less`},{label:`Severe/nephritis: pulse + triple therapy`,detail:`IV methylpred 250-1000 mg x3; MMF + belimumab or CNI; or CYC + belimumab`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Refractory: rituximab, anifrolumab`,detail:`Specialist; taper after 3-5 years remission`}]}]},"musculoskeletal-fractures-sports-injury":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`Describe a fracture by **bone and site, pattern, displacement, joint involvement and skin**.`,`A **sprain** is a ligament injury; a **strain** is a muscle-tendon injury.`,`**Overuse injury** is repetitive load exceeding repair: tendinopathy, apophysitis, stress fracture.`,`A **fragility fracture** over 50 is also a diagnosis of osteoporosis.`,`The mechanism predicts the fracture: fall on outstretched hand gives **Colles at 65, scaphoid at 25**.`,`The same fall gives a **supracondylar humerus fracture at 6**.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Gustilo-Anderson** open fractures: I under 1 cm, II 1-10 cm, III over 10 cm or high energy.`,`Grade IIIC has an **arterial injury** needing repair.`,`**Salter-Harris (SALTR)**: Slipped, Above, Lower, Through, Rammed.`,`Salter-Harris **II is commonest**; III-V are intra-articular and need referral.`,`Sprains: **grade I** stretch, **II** partial tear, **III** complete tear with no end point.`,`High-risk stress fractures: **femoral neck, anterior tibia, navicular, Jones**, pars.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Healing starts with **haematoma and inflammation** in the first week.`,`**Soft callus** forms at 2-3 weeks and **hard callus** at 6-12 weeks.`,`**Remodelling** to lamellar bone takes months to years.`,`Union: upper limb about **6 weeks**, lower limb about **12 weeks** in adults.`,`Swelling peaks at **48-72 hours**, so a tight cast can cause compartment syndrome.`,`Scaphoid blood flows **distal to proximal**, so the proximal pole can die.`,`Tendinopathy is a **failed healing response**, not inflammation.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask the **mechanism**, and whether the patient could **weight-bear 4 steps** at once.`,`**Swelling within 2 hours** suggests haemarthrosis from ACL tear or fracture.`,`Ask **numbness, cold limb** and pain escalating despite analgesia.`,`Ask **tetanus immunisation** history and any wound near the fracture.`,`Ask **why the patient fell**: syncope, hypoglycaemia, or a pathological fracture.`,`Overuse: ask about a sudden **training increase**, new shoes or surfaces.`,`In children, an inconsistent story suggests **non-accidental injury**.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`**Look, feel, move**, then document distal pulses, sensation and movement.`,`**Anatomical snuffbox tenderness** means treat as a scaphoid fracture.`,`**Simmonds-Thompson test**: no plantar flexion on calf squeeze means Achilles rupture.`,`**Lachman test** is the most sensitive test for ACL tear.`,`Always palpate the **proximal fibula** and the fifth metatarsal base in ankle injury.`,`Check fingers for **rotational malalignment** (scissoring) in hand fractures.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Apply **Ottawa and Canadian C-spine rules** before any X-ray.`,`X-ray **two views** at right angles, including the joint above and below.`,`Suspected scaphoid with normal film: splint and **MRI or repeat X-ray at 10-14 days**.`,`Suspected hip fracture with normal films: **MRI within 24 hours**.`,`Stress fractures are invisible on X-ray for **2-6 weeks**; order MRI.`,`Fragility fracture over 50: bloods and **DXA**.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Splint the **joint above and below** in the position of function.`,`Use a **back slab first** while swelling peaks; keep digits visible.`,`Hand safe position: **MCPs flexed 70-90 degrees**, IPs straight.`,`**Elevate for 48 hours**; return at once for rising pain, numbness or pale digits.`,`Soft tissue: **PEACE** for 1-3 days, then **LOVE** (load, optimism, vascularisation, exercise).`,`**Paracetamol first**; ibuprofen after 48 hours for 3-5 days.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Ankle sprain grade I-II: **brace or tape**, early walking, then balance training.`,`Tendinopathy: **progressive loading for 12 weeks**; eccentric heel drops for Achilles.`,`Low-risk stress fracture: relative rest for **4-8 weeks**, then graded return.`,`Short **tramadol or codeine** with a laxative only for severe pain.`,`**Enoxaparin 40 mg SC** daily in a lower-limb cast if VTE risk warrants.`,`**Never inject steroid** into or around the Achilles or patellar tendon.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`**Compartment syndrome**: pain out of proportion and **pain on passive stretch**.`,`Pulses persist until late; split the plaster to skin and keep at heart level.`,`**Open fracture**: IV antibiotics within 1 hour, saline dressing, splint, tetanus.`,`Child supracondylar fracture: check **radial pulse and the OK sign**.`,`**Complex regional pain syndrome** after cast removal needs early physiotherapy.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Plaster check at **24-48 hours**; X-ray at **7-10 days** for reducible fractures.`,`Fracture clinic **within 72 hours** for most confirmed fractures.`,`Same day: open, displaced intra-articular, neurovascular deficit, **Salter-Harris III-V**, hip.`,`Refer acute **Achilles rupture** and ACL tear with instability within days.`,`Return to play when strength is **at least 90%** of the other side.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for fractures and sports injury`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Paracetamol`,`1 g 4-6 hourly, max 4 g; child 15 mg/kg`,`First days, regularly`,`Liver toxicity; 3 g max if elderly`],[`Ibuprofen`,`400 mg oral TDS; child 10 mg/kg 8-hourly`,`3-5 days`,`GI bleed, AKI; avoid early soft tissue`],[`Diclofenac 1% gel`,`Topical 3-4 times daily`,`Up to 2 weeks`,`Local rash`],[`Tramadol`,`50-100 mg oral 4-6 hourly, max 400 mg`,`Under 1 week`,`Constipation, seizures; give laxative`],[`Co-amoxiclav (open fracture)`,`1.2 g IV 8-hourly`,`Within 1 hour, max 72 hours`,`Allergy; clindamycin if penicillin-allergic`],[`Cefazolin (open fracture)`,`2 g IV`,`Within 1 hour, until debridement`,`Allergy, diarrhoea`],[`Td vaccine`,`0.5 mL IM`,`Booster per wound type`,`Local pain; separate site from TIG`],[`Tetanus immunoglobulin`,`250 IU IM (500 IU if heavy contamination)`,`Single dose`,`Prone wound, incomplete immunisation`],[`Enoxaparin`,`40 mg SC once daily`,`Until lower-limb cast removed`,`Bleeding; reduce if eGFR under 30`]]},{heading:`Decision rules before imaging`,columns:[`Rule`,`Image if`,`Note`],rows:[[`Ottawa ankle`,`Posterior malleolar edge or tip tender, or no 4 steps`,`Nearly 100% sensitive`],[`Ottawa foot`,`Navicular or fifth metatarsal base tender`,`Palpate in every ankle injury`],[`Ottawa knee`,`55+, patella or fibular head tender, flexion under 90`,`Or cannot bear 4 steps`],[`Canadian C-spine`,`65+, dangerous mechanism, paraesthesia, cannot rotate 45 degrees`,`CT first line in adults`],[`Clinical scaphoid`,`Snuffbox or tubercle tenderness, thumb compression pain`,`Splint; MRI if X-ray normal`]]},{heading:`Common fractures in the clinic`,columns:[`Fracture`,`Immobilisation`,`Duration`,`Refer if`],rows:[[`Colles (distal radius)`,`Below-elbow back slab, then cast`,`About 6 weeks`,`Intra-articular, unstable, median nerve signs`],[`Scaphoid`,`Thumb spica or below-elbow cast`,`6-12 weeks`,`Displaced or proximal pole`],[`Clavicle (middle third)`,`Broad arm sling`,`3-4 weeks`,`Open, skin tenting, over 2 cm shortening`],[`Boxer (fifth metacarpal neck)`,`Ulnar gutter slab or buddy strapping`,`3-4 weeks`,`Rotation, fight bite`],[`Weber A ankle`,`Back slab, then walking boot`,`About 6 weeks`,`Bimalleolar or talar shift`],[`Fifth metatarsal base avulsion`,`Stiff-soled shoe or boot`,`4-6 weeks`,`Jones fracture`]]}],remember:[`Salter-Harris: SALTR - Slipped, Above, Lower, Through, Rammed.`,`Normal X-ray never excludes a scaphoid fracture.`,`Splint joint above and below, back slab first, digits visible.`,`Compartment syndrome: pain on passive stretch; pulses are a late sign.`,`RICE is now PEACE and LOVE.`,`Open fracture: antibiotics within 1 hour.`,`Tetanus-prone wound: Td if over 5 years; plus TIG if immunisation incomplete.`,`Stress fracture X-ray is normal for 2-6 weeks; get an MRI.`]},updated:[`Fragility fracture bone-health statements now cite NOGG 2024 (minor update of the 2021 guideline); content unchanged.`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Fractures, splinting and sports injury`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Fracture = break in bone continuity; **fragility fracture** = fall from standing height or less (osteoporosis assessment); sprain = ligament injury; tendinopathy = overload failure of tendon.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Gustilo-Anderson** open fractures I (under 1 cm), II (1-10 cm), IIIA/B/C; **Salter-Harris I-V** (SALTR) in children; ankle sprain grades I-III.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`FOOSH - **distal radius (elderly), scaphoid (young adult), supracondylar (child)**; osteoporosis, steroids, fluoroquinolones (tendon rupture), training errors (stress fracture).`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Haematoma -> inflammation -> soft callus -> hard callus -> remodelling; oedema peaks **48-72 h** -> rigid cast raises pressure -> **compartment syndrome**; retrograde scaphoid supply -> AVN.`],[`Key history`,`Mechanism, **weight-bearing 4 steps**, numbness/cold limb, pain out of proportion, wounds near fracture, fight bite, tetanus status, anticoagulants, steroids.`],[`Key examination`,`**Ottawa ankle/foot/knee rules**, snuffbox and scaphoid tubercle tenderness, rotational finger malalignment, distal neurovascular status, **pain on passive stretch**, skin over fracture.`],[`Investigations`,`Decision rules before X-ray; **two views at right angles incl. joint above and below**; suspected scaphoid with normal film - **MRI or repeat X-ray at 10-14 days**; CT C-spine if high risk.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Splint **joint above and below in a back slab** for 48-72 h, elevate, neurovascular chart; **paracetamol 1 g 4-6 hourly (max 4 g)**, ibuprofen 400 mg TDS 3-5 days after 48 h; soft tissue - **PEACE then LOVE**.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Short opioid (tramadol 50-100 mg, max 400 mg/day, with laxative); **fascia iliaca block** for hip fracture, haematoma block for Colles; fracture clinic within 72 h.`],[`Open fracture`,`**Antibiotics within 1 hour** (co-amoxiclav 1.2 g IV 8-hourly, cefuroxime 1.5 g or cefazolin 2 g) to debridement (max 72 h), tetanus, photograph, saline dressing, splint, same-day orthoplastic referral.`],[`Special groups`,`Children - physeal tenderness = Salter-Harris I, consider **non-accidental injury**; elderly - fragility fracture -> osteoporosis assessment; lower-limb cast with VTE risk - **enoxaparin 40 mg SC daily**.`],[`Complications`,`**Compartment syndrome**, neurovascular injury, infection, malunion, **scaphoid non-union/AVN**, CRPS, VTE, stiffness.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Pain out of proportion + pain on passive stretch - **split cast to skin, fasciotomy**; open fracture; cold pulseless limb; same day for displaced intra-articular, SH III-V, hip fracture.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Plaster check **24-48 h**, X-ray **7-10 days** for reducible fractures; tendinopathy - progressive loading 12 weeks; return to sport by function.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`Tetanus: **Td if under 3 doses; booster if over 10 years (clean) or 5 years (tetanus-prone)**, HTIG 250 IU; fracture liaison for fragility fractures (NOGG 2024).`],[`Latest guideline change`,`No change in this pass: **NOGG 2024** for fragility-fracture bone health; BOAST open fracture 2017 (debridement immediately if marine/agricultural/sewage, 12 h high-energy, 24 h others) and **PEACE & LOVE (BJSM 2020)** remain current.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Ottawa rules nearly 100% sensitive; **treat clinical scaphoid as fracture**; pulses persist in compartment syndrome; PEACE avoids early anti-inflammatories.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Limb injury in the clinic`,steps:[{label:`Open wound or skin tenting?`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Open fracture: antibiotics within 1 h`,detail:`Tetanus, saline dressing, splint, orthoplastic referral`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Neurovascular deficit or compartment signs?`,detail:`Pain on passive stretch; pulses may persist`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Apply Ottawa / scaphoid rules`,tone:`decision`},{label:`X-ray two views, joint above and below`,detail:`Clinical scaphoid: splint, MRI or repeat film 10-14 days`},{label:`Back slab, elevate, analgesia`,detail:`Plaster check 24-48 h; fracture clinic within 72 h`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Soft-tissue injury: PEACE then LOVE`,steps:[{label:`Days 1-3: PEACE`,detail:`Protect, Elevate, Avoid anti-inflammatories, Compress, Educate`},{label:`Analgesia: paracetamol first`,detail:`1 g 4-6 hourly, max 4 g/day; ibuprofen 400 mg TDS after 48 h if needed`},{label:`After day 3: LOVE`,detail:`Load, Optimism, Vascularisation (pain-free cardio), Exercise`},{label:`Progressive loading 6-12 weeks`,detail:`Imaging only if not improving`},{label:`Return to sport on function`,tone:`good`}]}]},"musculoskeletal-rheumatoid-arthritis":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Rheumatoid arthritis** is a chronic autoimmune, **symmetrical, erosive small-joint polyarthritis**.`,`It affects MCP, PIP, wrist and MTP joints and **spares the DIP joints**.`,`Morning stiffness lasts **over an hour**, with fatigue and extra-articular disease.`,`It affects about **0.5-0.75% of Indian adults**, women 3:1, peak onset 35-50.`,`About 70% are **RF and/or anti-CCP positive**.`,`**Cardiovascular disease** is the leading cause of death in RA.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**ACR/EULAR 2010**: needs at least one swollen joint not better explained otherwise.`,`Score joints (0-5), serology (0-3), acute-phase reactants (0-1), duration (0-1).`,`**6 or more of 10** is definite RA.`,`A score under 6 means **reassess**, not "no RA".`,`**DAS28**: remission under 2.6, low 2.6-3.2, moderate to 5.1, high over 5.1.`,`The lab-free **CDAI** (remission 2.8 or less) suits the OPD.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`**HLA-DRB1 shared epitope** presents citrullinated peptides to T cells.`,`**Smoking and periodontitis** drive citrullination; anti-CCP appears years before symptoms.`,`Macrophages and fibroblasts release **TNF-alpha, IL-6 and IL-1**, driving synovitis.`,`Invasive synovium (**pannus**) destroys cartilage with MMPs.`,`**RANKL** drives osteoclasts, causing **marginal erosions** first at MTP and MCP joints.`,`Treating within **3 months of symptoms** is the window of opportunity.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Morning stiffness **over 30-60 minutes** and pain easing with use suggest inflammation.`,`Ask **symmetry**, number of joints and whether DIPs are involved.`,`Synovitis under 6 weeks is often **viral** (chikungunya, parvovirus, hepatitis B).`,`Ask dry eyes, red painful eye, dry cough and **neck pain with hand tingling**.`,`Ask **TB history, hepatitis B and C, HIV** and vaccinations before DMARDs.`,`Ask **pregnancy plans**; methotrexate and leflunomide are teratogenic.`,`Ask about **co-trimoxazole**, which is dangerous with methotrexate.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Look for **ulnar deviation, swan-neck, boutonniere and Z-thumb**.`,`Feel for **boggy synovial swelling**, not hard bony swelling.`,`**MCP and MTP squeeze test**: pain suggests small-joint synovitis.`,`Do a **28-joint count** for DAS28.`,`Never force neck flexion; look for **UMN signs** of atlantoaxial subluxation.`,`Screen extra-articular: anaemia, scleritis, **lung crackles (ILD)**, splenomegaly, nodules.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Anti-CCP** is over 95% specific and predicts erosive disease.`,`**RF** is falsely positive in TB, leprosy, kala-azar and hepatitis C.`,`ESR, CRP, CBC, **creatinine, LFT and albumin** at baseline.`,`Screen **HBsAg, anti-HCV, HIV and chest X-ray** before methotrexate.`,`**IGRA or tuberculin test** before biologics or JAK inhibitors.`,`**X-ray hands and feet**: periarticular osteopenia and marginal erosions.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Start a DMARD **as soon as RA is diagnosed**; aim for remission.`,`**Methotrexate 15 mg once weekly**, rising to **20-25 mg** over weeks.`,`Write **"once weekly - on (day)"**; daily dosing has caused fatal aplasia.`,`**Folic acid 5 mg weekly** on a different day.`,`**Prednisolone 7.5-10 mg** as a bridge, stopped usually within 3 months.`,`Stop smoking, exercise, joint protection and **vaccinate before immunosuppression**.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`No improvement by **3 months** or target missed by **6 months**: change therapy.`,`If methotrexate is contraindicated, use **leflunomide or sulfasalazine** instead.`,`**Triple therapy** (methotrexate, sulfasalazine, hydroxychloroquine) is affordable in India.`,`Target missed on csDMARDs: **add a biologic** (EULAR 2025).`,`**JAK inhibitors** after risk review for MACE, malignancy and VTE.`,`Screen and treat **latent TB** at least 1 month before a biologic.`,`NSAIDs relieve symptoms but **do not modify disease**.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`Women stop **methotrexate at least 1 month** (traditionally 3) before conception.`,`Leflunomide needs a **cholestyramine washout** before conception.`,`Hydroxychloroquine, sulfasalazine and low-dose prednisolone are **safe in pregnancy**.`,`Elderly or eGFR 30-60: start methotrexate at **7.5-10 mg** weekly.`,`Surgery: continue methotrexate; **hold biologics** for one dosing interval.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Refer **suspected persistent synovitis urgently**, even if RF is negative.`,`Review every **1-3 months** until target, then every 3-6 months.`,`Bloods (BSR 2025): **week 2, monthly for 3-6 months**, then 3-monthly.`,`Withhold if **neutrophils under 1.6** or ALT over 100 U/L.`,`New dry cough on methotrexate: suspect **pneumonitis**, stop and image.`,`Annual review: CV risk **multiplied by 1.5**, FRAX, mood, vaccines, eye screening.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for rheumatoid arthritis`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Methotrexate`,`15 mg once weekly, up to 20-25 mg`,`Long term`,`Marrow, liver, pneumonitis; no co-trimoxazole`],[`Folic acid`,`5 mg once weekly, different day`,`With methotrexate`,`Reduces ulcers and liver rise`],[`Hydroxychloroquine`,`200-400 mg daily, max 5 mg/kg`,`Long term`,`Retinopathy; eye screening`],[`Sulfasalazine (EC)`,`500 mg daily, up to 1 g BD`,`Long term`,`Agranulocytosis, orange urine`],[`Leflunomide`,`10-20 mg once daily`,`Long term`,`Hypertension, liver; teratogenic`],[`Prednisolone (bridge)`,`7.5-10 mg each morning`,`Stop usually within 3 months`,`Sugar, bone loss; never stop abruptly`],[`Adalimumab`,`40 mg SC every 2 weeks`,`With methotrexate`,`TB reactivation, serious infection`],[`Tocilizumab`,`162 mg SC weekly or 8 mg/kg IV monthly`,`Long term`,`CRP falsely normal in infection`],[`Tofacitinib`,`5 mg oral BD`,`Long term`,`Zoster, VTE, MACE; avoid over 65 if possible`]]},{heading:`DAS28 and treat-to-target`,columns:[`DAS28`,`Activity`,`Action`],rows:[[`Under 2.6`,`Remission`,`Continue; stop steroids first after 6-12 months`],[`2.6 to 3.2`,`Low`,`Acceptable in long-standing disease`],[`Over 3.2 to 5.1`,`Moderate`,`Escalate; review every 1-3 months`],[`Over 5.1`,`High`,`Escalate; add a biologic if csDMARD fails`]]},{heading:`RA vs OA vs psoriatic arthritis in the hand`,columns:[`Feature`,`Rheumatoid arthritis`,`Osteoarthritis`,`Psoriatic arthritis`],rows:[[`Joints`,`MCP, PIP, wrist; DIP spared`,`DIP, PIP, thumb base`,`DIP, often asymmetric`],[`Swelling`,`Soft, boggy`,`Hard, bony (Heberden)`,`Sausage digit`],[`Morning stiffness`,`Over 1 hour`,`Under 30 minutes`,`Over 30 minutes`],[`Blood tests`,`RF, anti-CCP, raised CRP`,`Normal`,`RF negative`],[`X-ray`,`Marginal erosions, osteopenia`,`Osteophytes, narrowing`,`Pencil-in-cup`]]}],remember:[`RA is symmetrical small-joint polyarthritis that spares the DIPs.`,`ACR/EULAR 2010: 6 of 10 is definite RA.`,`Anti-CCP is specific; RF is falsely positive in TB, leprosy, kala-azar.`,`Methotrexate once weekly on a named day, never daily.`,`Never give co-trimoxazole with methotrexate.`,`Treat to target: change if no improvement at 3 months or target missed at 6.`,`Screen TB and hepatitis B before biologics; no live vaccines.`,`Multiply cardiovascular risk by 1.5.`]},updated:[`EULAR 2025: if target missed on methotrexate (with short-term steroid), add a biologic for everyone - poor prognostic factors no longer gate escalation; JAK inhibitor only after MACE, cancer and VTE risk review.`,`EULAR 2025: glucocorticoid bridge tapered and stopped as rapidly as clinically feasible; in sustained remission continuing the DMARD is preferred (tapering may still be considered).`,`BSR 2025 csDMARD guideline replaces BSR 2017: bloods at week 2, then monthly for 3-6 months, then 3-monthly, risk-stratified; folic acid at least 5 mg weekly; FIB-4 +/- elastography before methotrexate if liver risk.`,`BSR 2025: hydroxychloroquine retinal screening yearly after 5 years, or after 1 year if eGFR under 60, tamoxifen or dose over 5 mg/kg.`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Rheumatoid arthritis`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Chronic autoimmune **symmetrical erosive small-joint polyarthritis** (MCP, PIP, wrist, MTP; **DIP spared**), morning stiffness over 1 hour, extra-articular disease, raised CV mortality.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**ACR/EULAR 2010**: 1 swollen joint not better explained; joints 0-5, serology 0-3, APR 0-1, duration 6 weeks+ 0-1 - **6 of 10 = RA**; activity by **DAS28: remission under 2.6, low 2.6-3.2, high over 5.1**; CDAI 2.8 or less remission.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Women 3:1, age 35-50, HLA-DRB1 shared epitope, **smoking**, periodontitis (citrullination); India prevalence 0.5-0.75%.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Citrullinated peptides -> **anti-CCP** -> T/B cell synovitis -> **TNF, IL-6** -> pannus -> RANKL osteoclasts -> **marginal erosions** -> deformity; cytokines -> atherosclerosis.`],[`Key history`,`Symmetrical small-joint pain and swelling, **stiffness over 1 hour**, fatigue, dry eyes/mouth, breathlessness (ILD), pregnancy plans, **TB/HBV/HCV history**, smoking.`],[`Key examination`,`**Squeeze test of MCPs/MTPs**, boggy synovitis, 28-joint count, swan neck, boutonniere, Z-thumb, nodules; extra-articular - eyes, lungs, vasculitis; neck in long-standing RA.`],[`Investigations`,`**RF and anti-CCP (over 95% specific)**, ESR, CRP, CBC, creatinine, LFT, albumin, urine; X-ray hands and feet; HBsAg, anti-HBc, HCV, HIV, CXR; IGRA before biologic/JAKi; **FIB-4 if liver risk**.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Within 3 months: **methotrexate 15 mg once weekly** (7.5-10 mg elderly/eGFR 30-60), up 2.5-5 mg every 2-4 weeks to **20-25 mg**, + **folic acid at least 5 mg weekly** (not MTX day) + short prednisolone 7.5-10 mg bridge.`],[`Treatment - escalation (EULAR 2025)`,`No improvement at 3 months or target missed at 6: **add a biologic** (TNFi, tocilizumab, abatacept, rituximab) - **no prognostic-factor gate**; JAKi after **MACE/cancer/VTE risk** review; triple csDMARD where biologic unaffordable.`],[`Other csDMARDs`,`**HCQ 200-400 mg (5 mg/kg or less)**, **sulfasalazine 500 mg -> 1 g BD**, **leflunomide 10-20 mg** (teratogenic, washout cholestyramine 8 g TDS x 11 days); never MTX with **trimethoprim/co-trimoxazole**.`],[`How to take methotrexate`,`**Same named day weekly**, one tablet strength (2.5 mg), 'once weekly' written; missed dose - take within 2 days else skip; SC for GI intolerance; no alcohol excess.`],[`Special groups`,`**Pregnancy** - women stop MTX 1-3 months before (paternal low-dose use compatible); HCQ, SSZ, azathioprine, low-dose pred, certolizumab compatible; elderly - lower MTX; **CKD 3+ - higher MTX toxicity**, tighter monitoring.`],[`Complications`,`Erosions, deformity, **atlantoaxial subluxation**, ILD, scleritis, vasculitis, Felty, **CVD (risk x1.5)**, osteoporosis, infection incl. TB, lymphoma; drug toxicity (marrow, liver, pneumonitis).`],[`Red flags & referral`,`**Suspected persistent synovitis - urgent rheumatology referral even if RF negative**; fever/cough on biologic; single hot joint (sepsis); new dry cough on MTX (pneumonitis); neck pain with neurology.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring (BSR 2025)`,`Activity every 1-3 months until target then 3-6 monthly; **bloods at week 2, monthly for first 3-6 months, then every 3 months** (extend if stable low-risk); extra test at week 2 after dose rise; HCQ eyes yearly after 5 years.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`Stop smoking, dental care, inactivated vaccines (influenza, pneumococcal, HBV, recombinant zoster), **latent TB treatment (6H or 3HP) before biologics**, annual CV risk x1.5, FRAX.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**EULAR 2025**: biologic after csDMARD failure for all, JAKi after risk review, steroids stopped as fast as feasible, DMARD continuation preferred in remission; **BSR 2025** risk-based monitoring.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`DIP spared; anti-CCP specific; **MTX weekly never daily**; treat to target - change at 3 months if no improvement; RF positive in TB, leprosy, kala-azar, HCV.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Early inflammatory arthritis in the OPD`,steps:[{label:`Swollen joints, stiffness over 1 hour?`,detail:`Squeeze test MCPs/MTPs`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Urgent rheumatology referral`,detail:`Even if RF negative and ESR normal`,tone:`warn`},{label:`RF, anti-CCP, ESR, CRP, X-rays, baseline bloods`,detail:`HBV, HCV, HIV, CXR; FIB-4 if liver risk`},{label:`ACR/EULAR 2010 score 6 or more`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Start methotrexate + folic acid + bridge`,detail:`15 mg weekly up to 20-25 mg; pred 7.5-10 mg tapered fast`},{label:`Treat to target: DAS28 under 2.6`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`RA escalation (EULAR 2025)`,steps:[{label:`Phase 1: methotrexate + short steroid`,detail:`Leflunomide or sulfasalazine if MTX contraindicated`},{label:`Review 3 months, target by 6 months`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Phase 2: add a biologic DMARD`,detail:`TNFi, tocilizumab, abatacept or rituximab; triple csDMARD if unaffordable`},{label:`JAK inhibitor only after risk review`,detail:`Age 65+, smokers, CV, cancer or VTE risk - avoid if alternatives`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Phase 3: switch biologic or JAKi`,detail:`Same or different class`},{label:`Sustained remission: stop steroid first`,detail:`Prefer continuing DMARD; cautious taper possible`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`csDMARD monitoring (BSR 2025)`,columns:[`Drug`,`Bloods`,`Special checks`,`Stop or discuss if`],rows:[[`Methotrexate`,`Week 2, monthly 3-6 months, then 3-monthly`,`FIB-4 if liver risk; CXR baseline`,`Cytopenia, ALT rise, new cough`],[`Leflunomide`,`As methotrexate`,`BP and weight`,`Hypertension, ALT rise, neuropathy`],[`Sulfasalazine`,`As methotrexate`,`G6PD if at risk`,`Agranulocytosis, rash, hepatitis`],[`Hydroxychloroquine`,`None routine`,`Retina yearly after 5 years (1 year if high risk)`,`Retinopathy on OCT/fields`]]}]},"musculoskeletal-regional-soft-tissue-disorders":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`Pain from **tendons, sheaths, entheses, bursae, fascia or entrapped nerves**, not the joint itself.`,`Common in **IT workers, manual workers, farmers and homemakers** in India.`,`Most settle over **6-18 months**; 80-90% of tennis elbow and plantar fasciitis within a year.`,`**Diagnosis is clinical**: site of pain, focal tenderness and a provocation test.`,`Imaging is kept for atypical, refractory or red-flag cases.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Tendinopathy and enthesopathy**: tennis and golfer's elbow, Achilles, plantar fasciitis.`,`**Stenosing tenosynovitis**: de Quervain and trigger finger.`,`**Entrapment neuropathy**: carpal tunnel, cubital tunnel, meralgia, tarsal tunnel, Morton neuroma.`,`**Cysts**: the ganglion, arising from a joint capsule or tendon sheath.`,`CTS: mild intermittent, moderate frequent, **severe with thenar wasting** (needs surgery).`,`Trigger finger (Green) grades I-IV; **grade IV fixed contracture** goes to surgery.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`**Tendinopathy is failed healing, not inflammation**: disorganised collagen and new vessels.`,`**Progressive loading** remodels the tendon; rest and NSAIDs alone disappoint.`,`Tennis elbow involves the **ECRB origin** at the lateral epicondyle.`,`Entrapment goes from ischaemia (reversible) to demyelination to **axonal loss** (wasting).`,`CTS **spares the palm** because the palmar cutaneous branch runs over the retinaculum.`,`Stenosing tenosynovitis: a thickened sheath (**A1 pulley**) catches a tendon nodule.`,`Plantar fascia tightens overnight, causing **first-step pain**; the spur is not the cause.`]},{title:`Causes and risk factors`,points:[`Repetitive **gripping, wringing, grinding**, keyboard work and vibration.`,`**Diabetes**: trigger finger in 10-20% of diabetics.`,`**Hypothyroidism, acromegaly, RA, pregnancy and dialysis** predispose to CTS.`,`Barefoot standing on hard floors and weight gain cause plantar fasciitis.`,`**Fluoroquinolones** with steroids predispose to Achilles tendinopathy and rupture.`,`**Leprosy neuritis** mimics entrapment in India.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask the patient to **point with one finger** to the pain.`,`Tennis elbow: pain on **gripping and lifting palm-down**, age 35-55.`,`CTS: **night tingling** of thumb to radial ring finger, relieved by shaking the hand.`,`**Constant numbness or dropping things** means axonal loss in CTS.`,`De Quervain: radial wrist pain on **lifting a baby**; common in new mothers.`,`Trigger finger: painful **clicking or locking**, worse in the morning.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Tennis elbow: tenderness just below the lateral epicondyle; **Cozen test** positive.`,`CTS: **Tinel, Phalen and Durkan** (most sensitive); look for thenar wasting.`,`Cubital tunnel: **Froment sign**; dorsal ulnar hand numbness places the lesion at the elbow.`,`De Quervain: **Finkelstein test** reproduces pain over the radial styloid.`,`Plantar fasciitis: tender **medial calcaneal tubercle**, positive windlass test.`,`**Always feel the ulnar nerve** for thickening and look for anaesthetic patches (leprosy).`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**No test needed** for typical tennis elbow, de Quervain, trigger finger or plantar fasciitis.`,`**HbA1c and TSH** in CTS, trigger finger and recurrent problems.`,`**Nerve conduction studies** before surgery, if uncertain, or with weakness.`,`**Ultrasound** confirms CTS, plantar fascia thickening, ganglion or tendon tear.`,`A heel spur on X-ray is **not a diagnosis**.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Relative rest and load modification**, not immobilisation.`,`**Progressive exercise** is the core treatment for tendinopathy.`,`**Neutral wrist splint at night for 6-8 weeks** for CTS.`,`**Thumb spica for 3-6 weeks** for de Quervain.`,`Plantar fasciitis: **calf and fascia stretching**, cushioned shoes, no barefoot walking.`,`**Topical diclofenac gel** first; short oral NSAID course if needed.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Steroid injection: **trigger finger and de Quervain** respond well (60-90%).`,`Tennis elbow injection helps at 6 weeks but is **worse at 1 year**.`,`Maximum **three injections a year** at one site, 6 weeks apart.`,`**Never inject** into or around the Achilles or patellar tendon.`,`Diabetics check **blood glucose for 2-5 days** after injection.`,`**Carpal tunnel release** for thenar wasting, constant numbness or failed care.`,`**A1 pulley release** after two failed injections or a fixed flexion.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review at **6-12 weeks**; reconsider diagnosis if no progress by 3-6 months.`,`Urgent: **Kanavel signs** of septic tenosynovitis, tendon rupture, a hard lump over 5 cm.`,`Half of ganglia **disappear on their own**; never hit one with a book.`,`Refer **multiple enthesitis with inflammatory back pain** to rheumatology.`,`Treat **leprosy neuritis** under NLEP with prednisolone and MDT.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs and injections for soft-tissue disorders`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Diclofenac 1% gel`,`2-4 g topical 3-4 times daily`,`2-4 weeks`,`Local rash; wash hands`],[`Paracetamol`,`500 mg-1 g oral up to QID`,`Short term`,`Max 4 g/day; safe in pregnancy`],[`Ibuprofen / naproxen`,`400 mg TDS / 250-500 mg BD oral`,`7-14 days`,`GI bleed, AKI; PPI if over 65`],[`Methylprednisolone acetate`,`CTS 40 mg; elbow or heel 20-40 mg`,`Max 3 a year per site`,`Skin atrophy, flare, high sugars`],[`Triamcinolone acetonide`,`10-20 mg into sheath`,`Trigger finger, de Quervain`,`More fat atrophy, depigmentation`],[`Lignocaine 1%`,`0.5-2 mL mixed with steroid`,`Per injection`,`Aspirate first; transient numbness`],[`Prednisolone (leprosy neuritis)`,`40 mg daily, taper every 2 weeks`,`12 weeks`,`Screen TB, diabetes, Strongyloides`]]},{heading:`Tendon and fascia disorders`,columns:[`Disorder`,`Structure`,`Classic story`,`Test`],rows:[[`Tennis elbow`,`ECRB origin, lateral epicondyle`,`Pain gripping, lifting palm-down`,`Cozen, Mill`],[`Golfer's elbow`,`Flexor-pronator origin`,`Medial elbow pain on gripping`,`Resisted wrist flexion`],[`De Quervain`,`APL and EPB, first compartment`,`Radial wrist pain lifting a baby`,`Finkelstein`],[`Trigger finger`,`Flexor tendon at A1 pulley`,`Clicking, morning locking`,`Tender moving nodule`],[`Plantar fasciitis`,`Fascia at medial calcaneal tubercle`,`First-step heel pain`,`Windlass test`],[`Achilles tendinopathy`,`Midportion or insertion`,`Morning stiffness, loading pain`,`Simmonds-Thompson for rupture`]]},{heading:`Nerve entrapments`,columns:[`Syndrome`,`Nerve and site`,`Symptoms`,`Key test`],rows:[[`Carpal tunnel`,`Median, under flexor retinaculum`,`Night tingling radial 3.5 fingers`,`Tinel, Phalen, Durkan`],[`Cubital tunnel`,`Ulnar, behind medial epicondyle`,`Little and ring finger, dorsum too`,`Froment, elbow flexion test`],[`Guyon canal`,`Ulnar at wrist`,`Ulnar digits, dorsum spared`,`Intrinsic weakness`],[`Meralgia paraesthetica`,`Lateral femoral cutaneous at ASIS`,`Burning lateral thigh, no weakness`,`Tinel medial to ASIS`],[`Tarsal tunnel`,`Tibial, behind medial malleolus`,`Burning sole on standing`,`Tinel behind malleolus`],[`Morton neuroma`,`Interdigital nerve, third web space`,`Burning forefoot`,`Mulder click`]]}],remember:[`Tendinopathy is degeneration, not inflammation: load it, do not rest it.`,`Tennis elbow injection: better at 6 weeks, worse at 1 year.`,`CTS spares the palm; thenar wasting means surgery.`,`Cubital tunnel numbs the dorsal ulnar hand; Guyon canal spares it.`,`Feel the ulnar nerve in India: leprosy mimics entrapment.`,`Never inject the Achilles; max three injections a year at one site.`,`Plantar fasciitis is first-step pain; the spur is not the cause.`]},master:{heading:`Master revision table - Regional soft-tissue disorders`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Localised, mostly degenerative overuse conditions of **tendons, entheses, fascia and entrapped nerves** - tennis elbow, carpal tunnel, de Quervain, trigger finger, ganglion, plantar fasciitis, Achilles tendinopathy.`],[`Relevant classification`,`By tissue: **tendinopathy/enthesopathy**, **stenosing tenosynovitis** (de Quervain, trigger finger), **entrapment neuropathy** (CTS, cubital, Guyon, meralgia, tarsal tunnel), cysts (ganglion), bursitis.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Repetitive gripping, typing, new mothers lifting babies (de Quervain), **diabetes** (trigger finger, CTS), hypothyroidism, pregnancy, obesity and standing work (plantar fasciitis), **leprosy** (nerve thickening).`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Overload -> **tendinosis** (degenerative, not inflammatory) -> neovessels and pain; CTS: raised tunnel pressure -> venous congestion -> demyelination -> **axonal loss (wasting)**.`],[`Key history`,`Pain on gripping palm-down (tennis elbow), **night paraesthesia of radial 3.5 fingers** (CTS), constant numbness or dropping objects, first-step morning heel pain, triggering, skin patches (leprosy).`],[`Key examination`,`**Cozen, Mill, Maudsley**; **Tinel, Phalen, Durkan** (most sensitive), thenar wasting; **Finkelstein**; Windlass and calcaneal tubercle tenderness; palpate the **ulnar nerve for thickening**.`],[`Investigations`,`**Clinical diagnosis** - no test for typical cases; ultrasound (median nerve CSA over 10-12 mm2, plantar fascia over 4 mm); **NCS if atypical or before surgery**; slit-skin smear if leprosy suspected.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Activity modification, **progressive loading exercise**, splints (**neutral night wrist splint 6-8 weeks** CTS, thumb spica 3-6 weeks de Quervain, night ankle splint), counterforce brace, calf/plantar fascia stretching.`],[`Treatment - drugs`,`Paracetamol 500 mg-1 g QID; **ibuprofen 400 mg TDS or naproxen 250-500 mg BD 7-14 days** + PPI if at risk; **CTS - NSAIDs, diuretics, pyridoxine, gabapentin do not help**.`],[`Devices / procedures (injections)`,`**Trigger finger/de Quervain triamcinolone 10-20 mg**; **CTS methylprednisolone 40 mg**; tennis elbow and plantar fascia methylprednisolone 20-40 mg (medial approach); **max 3/year/site**; never into Achilles.`],[`Treatment - escalation (surgery)`,`**Carpal tunnel release** (over 90% success) for wasting, constant numbness, severe NCS; **A1 pulley release** after 2 failed injections; first compartment release; ganglion - reassure, aspirate, excise.`],[`Special groups`,`**Pregnancy** - CTS and de Quervain settle after delivery, splints and paracetamol; **diabetics** - check sugars 2-5 days after injection, lower injection success; paediatric trigger thumb - release at 2-3 years.`],[`Complications`,`Tendon rupture, fat atrophy and depigmentation after steroid, recurrence (tennis elbow worse at 6-12 months after steroid), permanent thenar wasting.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`**Septic tenosynovitis (Kanavel signs)**, tendon rupture, thenar wasting, **hard fixed lump or over 5 cm**, thickened nerves with anaesthetic patches (leprosy - **prednisolone 40 mg tapered over 12 weeks + MDT**).`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Review **6-12 weeks**; if no progress by 3-6 months reconsider diagnosis and move up the ladder.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`No change in this pass: **AAOS CTS CPG 2024**, NICE CKS 2023, JOSPT heel pain 2023 and NLEP roadmap 2023-2027 remain current.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Tendinopathy is degenerative - **load it, do not rest it**; Durkan is the most sensitive CTS test; **always feel the ulnar nerve in India**; steroid helps tennis elbow short term but worsens 1-year outcome.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Hand numbness: carpal tunnel or something else`,steps:[{label:`Night paraesthesia of radial 3.5 fingers`,detail:`Palm spared, shaking relieves`},{label:`Thickened nerves or anaesthetic patches?`,detail:`Leprosy neuritis - steroids and MDT`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Neck pain or dermatomal pattern?`,detail:`C6-7 radiculopathy`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Tinel, Phalen, Durkan positive`,detail:`Check thenar wasting, TSH, HbA1c`},{label:`Wasting or constant numbness?`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Yes - NCS and carpal tunnel release`,tone:`warn`},{label:`No - night splint 6-8 weeks, then injection`,detail:`Methylprednisolone 40 mg`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Soft-tissue disorder treatment ladder`,steps:[{label:`Explain, modify activity`,detail:`Avoid provoking grip or load`},{label:`Progressive loading exercise and splint`,detail:`6-12 weeks; stretching for plantar fascia`},{label:`Short NSAID or paracetamol`,detail:`Ibuprofen 400 mg TDS 7-14 days; not for CTS`},{label:`Steroid injection`,detail:`Trigger finger 10-20 mg triamcinolone; CTS 40 mg methylprednisolone; max 3/year`},{label:`Surgery after failure`,detail:`CTS release, A1 pulley release, first compartment release`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Tendon disorders: site and test`,columns:[`Condition`,`Site`,`Key test`,`First-line care`],rows:[[`Tennis elbow`,`ECRB origin, lateral epicondyle`,`Cozen, Mill, Maudsley`,`Loading exercise, brace`],[`De Quervain`,`APL and EPB, first compartment`,`Finkelstein (Eichhoff)`,`Thumb spica, injection`],[`Trigger finger`,`A1 pulley`,`Palpable nodule, triggering`,`Injection 10-20 mg`],[`Plantar fasciitis`,`Medial calcaneal tubercle`,`Windlass, first-step pain`,`Stretching, night splint`]]}]}};export{e as default};